#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""pervio-orchestrate — run a small multi-step agent workflow, detached.

P2 (linear workflows). A workflow is an ordered list of steps; each step runs one
agent turn via pervio-chat (Claude or Codex), and a later step can reference an
earlier step's output with `{{stepId.output}}` in its prompt. The whole run is
DETACHED (its own session, like pervio-run) so it survives the phone disconnecting,
and its state lives in a plain-JSON ledger the phone polls. No daemon: the runner
process exits when the workflow finishes.

Spec (JSON), passed base64 to `start`:
  {
    "name": "thing",
    "dir": "/abs/project/dir",            # default working dir for steps
    "setup": "npm ci",                    # optional: provision each fresh worktree
    "steps": [
      {"id":"plan","backend":"claude","perm":"plan","prompt":"…"},
      {"id":"impl","backend":"codex","sandbox":"workspace-write",
       "prompt":"Implement this plan:\n{{plan.output}}"}
    ]
  }

Worktree isolation (P3): when the workflow dir is a git repo and the DAG contains
two or more WRITE steps that can run concurrently (neither reaches the other via
dependsOn ∪ {{id.output}} refs), every in-repo step runs in its own git worktree
branched from an integration branch (pervio/wf-<wfId>/integration; step branches
pervio/wf-<wfId>/steps/<id> — same-depth siblings, refs D/F-safe). The orchestrator
commits each write step's changes, gates them on `verify`, then serially merges
into the integration branch (a conflict fails the LOSING step with the file list;
its branch is kept). If every step succeeds and the user's checkout still sits on
the same branch+commit as at start, the integration branch is fast-forwarded back;
otherwise the branch is left and ledger.git says so. Fresh worktrees contain only
COMMITTED state (no node_modules/.env) — the optional top-level `setup` command
runs in each worktree before its agent turn. Read steps (plan / read-only sandbox,
no verify) run --detach'd; if one writes anyway its work is rescue-committed and
the step fails (fail-closed classification: anything else counts as a writer).
Workflows without concurrent writers run EXACTLY as before (no git ops, no new
ledger fields). Deleting a run prunes its worktrees and deletes its branches.

Layout, one dir per workflow run:
  ~/.pervio/wf/<wfId>/
    spec.json        # the submitted spec
    ledger.json      # {wfId,name,dir,status,startedAt,steps:[{id,backend,status,
                     #  output,error,bytes}]}  — the phone reads this
    <stepId>.jsonl   # that step's raw stream-json (pervio-chat output)
    status           # "running" -> "exited:<code>"
    pgid

Command steps: a step with a `command` runs that shell line (bash -lc, in the step
dir) INSTEAD of an agent turn — its stdout becomes the step's output. Deterministic
and token-free; the data layer of scheduled digests. {{id.output}} refs are NOT
substituted into a command (upstream agent text into a shell line is an injection
vector) — a command is a fixed string, same trust level as `verify`. Output is read
incrementally into a BOUNDED buffer: CMD_MAX_OUTPUT bytes are kept, the rest is
drained (so a big-but-finite command still finishes and reports its own exit code)
up to CMD_MAX_DRAIN, past which the command is a firehose — its process group is
killed and the step fails saying so, instead of the runner buffering it all in RAM.

Publish: a step with `publish: true` (or `publish: "<dir>"`) is the workflow's
publication — the moment that STEP succeeds (not the whole run), its final output
is archived as today's issue, <dir>/<YYYY-MM-DD>.md (default dir
~/.pervio/archives/<slug-of-name>/), written atomically; a same-day rerun
overwrites. Empty output publishes nothing (a quiet day must not become a blank
issue), and a write failure never fails an otherwise-good step (runner.err says
why). The ledger step records publishedTo; `archives list` folds publish dirs in
beside the legacy display.archiveDir compat layer. At most one step per spec.

Schedules: `schedule` fires a saved spec on a daily clock with no daemon. The OS
scheduler (macOS LaunchAgent / Linux crontab, registered once by `schedule add`)
invokes `schedule tick` every 5 minutes; tick is the brain: at-most-once per due
period (catch-up after sleep/reboot fires the most recent missed period only —
the state re-read, the launch and the period claim all happen inside the schedule's
own lock, the one every phone verb takes, so a pause can never be raced by a start),
an overlap guard that checks the previous run's LIVENESS (pgid, not just ledger
text — a reboot mid-run must not wedge the schedule), 3 start attempts per period,
and pruning of old runs past keepRuns (via the normal delete, so conversations
sweep too). State: ~/.pervio/schedules/<id>/{spec.json,schedule.json}. The hook
exists only while >=1 enabled schedule exists (zero wakeups otherwise). Tests
inject time via PERVIO_TICK_NOW, skip OS registration via PERVIO_SCHED_HOOK=skip,
and pick the hook flavour via PERVIO_SCHED_HOOK_KIND=darwin|cron (CI runs on Linux).

Params (F2): `start --params-b64 <b64(JSON object name->value)>` mechanically
substitutes {{param.<name>}} placeholders — into each step's `prompt` and the
top-level spec `dir` ONLY, never into `command`/`verify`/anything else (a param
value in a shell line is an injection vector). Values must be str/int/bool. Any
{{param. left ANYWHERE in the spec after substitution (a placeholder in a
command, an undeclared name, a value smuggling a new placeholder in) rejects the
whole start: params only substitute into prompts and dir. Substitution runs
BEFORE _validate_spec, so a substituted dir goes through the same _dir_ok
'..'-traversal check as a literal one. This layer is mechanical on purpose —
required/default/type declarations live in the template WRAPPER and are enforced
by pervio-mcp (which holds the wrapper); a direct CLI caller owns its values.

Commands:
  pervio-orchestrate start  --spec-b64 B64 [--params-b64 B64] [--resume-from WFID] [--device ID --origin ENTRY]
  pervio-orchestrate teststep --wf WFID --step ID [--item N] [--spec-b64 B64]
  pervio-orchestrate status --wf WFID
  pervio-orchestrate tail   --wf WFID          # stream ledger.json snapshots
  pervio-orchestrate list
  pervio-orchestrate spec   --wf WFID
  pervio-orchestrate delete --wf WFID
  pervio-orchestrate run    --wf WFID          # internal: the detached runner
  pervio-orchestrate schedule {add|update|list|remove|enable|disable|fire|tick|install|uninstall}
"""
import base64
import concurrent.futures
import datetime
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import threading
import time
import uuid

MAX_PARALLEL = int(os.environ.get("PERVIO_MAX_PARALLEL", "4") or "4")   # cap concurrent agent turns
CHILD_TIMEOUT = float(os.environ.get("PERVIO_CHILD_TIMEOUT", "180") or "180")
CMD_TIMEOUT = float(os.environ.get("PERVIO_CMD_TIMEOUT", "600") or "600")   # command steps
# Cap a command step's captured stdout: it goes into ledger.json (rewritten whole
# on every save) and is streamed to the phone, so an unbounded `cat huge` must not
# bloat state or the tail. THE CONTRACT (2026-09-02): this is a DATA cap, and
# hitting it is an event, not a shrug — the capture gets CMD_TRUNC_MARK appended,
# and any mechanical consumer (fanout.over) REFUSES a marked source instead of
# quietly working a partial list. 4 MiB because the old 256 KiB carried a stale
# assumption ("far more than any digest needs" — true at 80 fetched papers,
# false the day the fetch cap went to 500: 19 shards, 433 KB, 7 shards silently
# never dispatched, 92 papers lost).
CMD_MAX_OUTPUT = int(os.environ.get("PERVIO_CMD_MAX_OUTPUT", str(4 * 1024 * 1024)) or str(4 * 1024 * 1024))
# The marker a truncated capture ends with. Written in ONE place, checked by the
# fanout guard — grep for it before inventing a second spelling.
CMD_TRUNC_MARK = "…[truncated"
# Past the kept cap the pipe is still DRAINED (a big-but-finite command must still
# finish and report its own exit code) — but only this far. Beyond it the command is
# a firehose (`yes`, a runaway loop, a tail -f): its process group is killed and the
# step fails saying so, instead of the runner buffering gigabytes for the whole
# CMD_TIMEOUT and then reporting an empty output.
#
# The ceiling is 256 MiB, not 8 MiB: draining costs no memory (the keep-buffer is
# already bounded by CMD_MAX_OUTPUT), so the only thing this limit buys is not
# spending CMD_TIMEOUT reading from something that will never stop. An 8 MiB
# ceiling bought that at the price of KILLING legitimate finite commands — a
# `git log -p` on a fat repo, a data dump — which used to succeed with a truncated
# capture. A firehose still trips this in seconds; a real command never does.
CMD_MAX_DRAIN = int(os.environ.get("PERVIO_CMD_MAX_DRAIN", str(256 * 1024 * 1024))
                    or str(256 * 1024 * 1024))
# stderr is only ever quoted as a 300-char tail; keep a small rolling window of it.
CMD_ERR_TAIL = 8192
# Top-level agent turns and user-authored setup/verify shells used to be un-timed
# subprocess.call — a hang (or `verify: sleep 999999`) stranded the runner thread,
# left the run 'running' forever, and >6h later tricked the scheduler into an
# overlapping start. Bound all three like command steps do. Agent turns get a
# generous ceiling (a real review/digest turn is legitimately long); setup/verify
# reuse the command-step budget since they're the same bash -lc shape.
AGENT_TIMEOUT = float(os.environ.get("PERVIO_AGENT_TIMEOUT", "1800") or "1800")
SETUP_TIMEOUT = float(os.environ.get("PERVIO_SETUP_TIMEOUT", str(CMD_TIMEOUT)) or str(CMD_TIMEOUT))
VERIFY_TIMEOUT = float(os.environ.get("PERVIO_VERIFY_TIMEOUT", str(CMD_TIMEOUT)) or str(CMD_TIMEOUT))
# Hard ceiling on a fan-out step's item count. A spec (or an upstream agent that
# generates one) could otherwise set fanout.max arbitrarily high; children run
# maxParallel-at-a-time but the TOTAL is unbounded and doesn't hit the P4 dispatch
# budget. 200 is far above any real template (deep-audit uses 8).
FANOUT_MAX_CAP = int(os.environ.get("PERVIO_FANOUT_MAX_CAP", "200") or "200")

# Set per-run by cmd_run to <rd>/live. Agent/command/fan-out children each run in
# their OWN session (so a per-child timeout can killpg just that child's subtree
# without taking down the runner) — which also means killing the RUNNER's pgid does
# NOT reap them. Each in-flight child registers its pgid here so _delete_run can
# reap the ones still alive, with the same reused-pid safety token _wf_alive uses.
_LIVE_DIR = None

os.umask(0o077)   # workflow state may contain prompts/outputs — owner-only

HOME = os.path.expanduser("~")
WF = os.path.join(HOME, ".pervio", "wf")
SCHED = os.path.join(HOME, ".pervio", "schedules")
SELF = os.path.realpath(__file__)
SELF_DIR = os.path.dirname(SELF)
CHAT = os.path.join(SELF_DIR, "pervio-chat")
PUSH = os.path.join(SELF_DIR, "pervio-push")


def _push_notify(title, body, thread="", device=""):
    """Fire a lock-screen push at the phone, fire-and-forget. This is a courtesy
    ping (a publish landed, a run failed, an approval is waiting) — it must NEVER
    delay or fail the workflow, so it is fully detached, output-swallowed, and
    every error here (including pervio-push not existing) is caught and dropped.
    pervio-push itself silently no-ops when the APNs key/token aren't set up yet,
    so this stays quiet until the user provisions the key. See docs/apns.md."""
    if not (title or body):
        return
    try:
        subprocess.Popen(
            [sys.executable, PUSH, "send", "--title", title, "--body", body]
            + (["--thread", thread] if thread else [])
            # Whoever started the workflow is the one told about it (the phone's
            # push device id, recorded at start); without one every phone on
            # this Mac was buzzed (谁发的推给谁 for workflows too, 2026-10-18).
            + (["--device", device] if device else []),
            stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,
            stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, start_new_session=True)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        pass

sys.path.insert(0, SELF_DIR)
from _pervio_lib import clamp_tier, run_child, DispatchError, push_lang, resolve_model, models_menu   # noqa: E402


# The words of every push this script sends, in the phone's language. Pure
# functions on the pervio-run `_push_text` model: a body you can assert in a test
# without running a workflow. `lang=None` reads the synced preference
# (~/.pervio/push/lang, written by the app beside its APNs token) at PUSH time;
# tests pass it explicitly. zh strings are the shipped ones, byte-for-byte.

def _push_copy_published(date_str, lang=None):
    return date_str + (" delivered" if (lang or push_lang()) == "en" else " 已送达")


def _push_copy_failed(failed, lang=None):
    step = sorted(failed)[0]
    if (lang or push_lang()) == "en":
        return "Failed: " + step + ((" (%d steps)" % len(failed)) if len(failed) > 1 else "")
    return "失败:" + step + ((" 等 %d 步" % len(failed)) if len(failed) > 1 else "")


def _push_copy_done(n, lang=None):
    return ("All %d steps finished" % n) if (lang or push_lang()) == "en" else ("%d 步全部完成" % n)

VALID_ID = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z0-9_-]+$")
REF_RE = re.compile(r"\{\{\s*([A-Za-z0-9_-]+)\.output\s*\}\}")
# The structured lane (WF-1): {{id.json}} is a step's PARSED value — canonical
# JSON, machine-shaped, no FAIL markers or progress headers. Only steps that
# actually have one (fanout, or an agent step with outputSchema) may be
# referenced this way; _validate_spec enforces it at the door.
JSON_REF_RE = re.compile(r"\{\{\s*([A-Za-z0-9_-]+)\.json\s*\}\}")
ANY_REF_RE = re.compile(r"\{\{\s*([A-Za-z0-9_-]+)\.(output|json)\s*\}\}")
# Appended to the ORIGINAL prompt for the one corrective re-run when a step's
# output flunks its outputSchema. One retry, never a loop: a model that can't
# fix its format on a direct complaint won't fix it on the tenth.
SCHEMA_FIX_NOTE = ("\n\n[格式纠错] 你上一次的输出不符合约定的 JSON 契约:%s\n"
                   "上次输出开头:%s\n"
                   "重新输出:只输出符合契约的 JSON,不要解释,不要代码栅栏。")
# "item": {{item}} is the fan-out per-item placeholder. "param": a step named
# param would make {{param.output}} ambiguous between "that step's output" and
# the F2 parameter namespace {{param.<name>}} — reserve it outright.
RESERVED_IDS = frozenset({"item", "param"})
# F2 template parameters: {{param.<name>}}, name ^[a-z][a-z0-9_]{0,23}$ (matches
# the wrapper declaration grammar pervio-mcp validates). The LEFTOVER probe is
# deliberately looser (any "{{param." prefix): a malformed name that the
# substitution regex can never satisfy must reject the start, not ride through.
PARAM_NAME = re.compile(r"^[a-z][a-z0-9_]{0,23}$")
PARAM_RE = re.compile(r"\{\{\s*param\.([a-z][a-z0-9_]{0,23})\s*\}\}")
PARAM_LEFTOVER = re.compile(r"\{\{\s*param\.")
# Failure strings worth ONE automatic retry: infrastructure weather (a dropped
# connection, an overloaded API, a wall-clock timeout), not the agent being wrong.
# A 6am schedule has nobody watching to tap "run again" — one retry absorbs the
# blip; a second failure is reported as-is (never a retry loop). Top-level
# claude/codex steps only: command steps are deterministic (same input, same
# failure) and fan-out children have their own tolerance knobs.
TRANSIENT_RE = re.compile(r"timed out|timeout|connection closed|api error"
                          r"|overloaded|529|econnreset", re.I)


def _prompt_refs(prompt):
    """The {{id.output}} / {{id.json}} refs in a text — a step's data inputs
    (flow edges), distinct from dependsOn (timing). Order-preserving,
    de-duplicated. BOTH kinds are edges: a consumer of a step's parsed value
    races its producer exactly as hard as a consumer of its text."""
    out = []
    for m in ANY_REF_RE.finditer(prompt or ""):
        if m.group(1) not in out:
            out.append(m.group(1))
    return out


# ---------- structured outputs (WF-1) ----------

def _json_text(v):
    """A structured value as prompt/stdin text — ONE canonical spelling
    everywhere, so a downstream `cat -` sees exactly what a prompt sees."""
    return json.dumps(v, ensure_ascii=False)


def _parse_structured(text):
    """Best-effort JSON out of real LLM output: strip a surrounding code fence,
    then parse; failing that, parse the outermost [...] or {...} span (models
    love a 'Sure! Here you go:' preamble). Returns (value, "") or (None, err).
    A bare JSON scalar only counts when the whole reply is that scalar —
    fishing a number out of prose would 'succeed' on anything."""
    s = re.sub(r"(?s)^```[a-zA-Z]*\s*|\s*```$", "", (text or "").strip()).strip()
    try:
        return json.loads(s), ""
    except ValueError:
        m = re.search(r"(?s)(\[.*\]|\{.*\})", s)
        if m:
            try:
                return json.loads(m.group(1)), ""
            except ValueError:
                pass
        return None, "not valid JSON"


def _schema_check(schema, value, path="$"):
    """Validate against the SUPPORTED SUBSET of JSON Schema — stdlib only, no
    dependency: type (object/array/string/number/integer/boolean/null),
    properties + required (unknown keys pass), items, enum, minItems/maxItems,
    minLength/maxLength. Returns "" when valid, else the FIRST complaint with
    its path — one precise sentence beats a wall, both for the corrective
    retry prompt and for the ledger error."""
    if not isinstance(schema, dict):
        return ""                    # an empty/absent schema constrains nothing
    t = schema.get("type")
    if t is not None:
        checks = {"object": lambda v: isinstance(v, dict),
                  "array": lambda v: isinstance(v, list),
                  "string": lambda v: isinstance(v, str),
                  "number": lambda v: isinstance(v, (int, float)) and not isinstance(v, bool),
                  "integer": lambda v: isinstance(v, int) and not isinstance(v, bool),
                  "boolean": lambda v: isinstance(v, bool),
                  "null": lambda v: v is None}
        fn = checks.get(t)
        if fn and not fn(value):
            return "%s: expected %s, got %s" % (path, t, type(value).__name__)
    if "enum" in schema and value not in schema["enum"]:
        return "%s: %r is not one of %s" % (path, value, schema["enum"])
    if isinstance(value, str):
        if "minLength" in schema and len(value) < schema["minLength"]:
            return "%s: shorter than minLength %s" % (path, schema["minLength"])
        if "maxLength" in schema and len(value) > schema["maxLength"]:
            return "%s: longer than maxLength %s" % (path, schema["maxLength"])
    if isinstance(value, list):
        if "minItems" in schema and len(value) < schema["minItems"]:
            return "%s: fewer than minItems %s" % (path, schema["minItems"])
        if "maxItems" in schema and len(value) > schema["maxItems"]:
            return "%s: more than maxItems %s" % (path, schema["maxItems"])
        items = schema.get("items")
        if isinstance(items, dict):
            for i, v in enumerate(value):
                bad = _schema_check(items, v, "%s[%d]" % (path, i))
                if bad:
                    return bad
    if isinstance(value, dict):
        for k in (schema.get("required") or []):
            if k not in value:
                return "%s: missing required key %r" % (path, k)
        props = schema.get("properties")
        if isinstance(props, dict):
            for k, sub in props.items():
                if k in value:
                    bad = _schema_check(sub, value[k], "%s.%s" % (path, k))
                    if bad:
                        return bad
    return ""


def _parse_and_validate(schema, text):
    """(parsed value, "") when the text parses AND conforms; (None, complaint)
    otherwise. The complaint is what the corrective retry quotes back."""
    val, err = _parse_structured(text)
    if err:
        return None, err
    bad = _schema_check(schema, val)
    return (None, bad) if bad else (val, "")


def _write_json_artifact(rd, sid, value):
    """A step's parsed value at <rd>/json/<sid>.json — the file teststep and
    resume read back, and the thing {{sid.json}} serializes. Atomic like every
    other run-state write."""
    jd = os.path.join(rd, "json")
    os.makedirs(jd, mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
    _write_atomic(os.path.join(jd, "%s.json" % sid), value)


def _resolve_refs(text, snap, snap_json, keep_ids=(), repoint=None):
    """Substitute {{id.output}} (text, optionally worktree-repointed) and
    {{id.json}} (canonical JSON of the parsed value) in one pass. keep_ids
    are left VERBATIM — the fan-out item placeholder ({{item.output}} names a
    field of the item, not a step) must survive to the per-item pass."""
    def sub(m):
        pid, kind = m.group(1), m.group(2)
        if pid in keep_ids:
            return m.group(0)
        if kind == "json":
            v = snap_json.get(pid)
            return _json_text(v) if v is not None else ""
        t = snap.get(pid, "")
        return repoint(pid, t) if repoint else t
    return ANY_REF_RE.sub(sub, text or "")


# ---------- worktree isolation helpers ----------

# Hook-free identity for every git op the orchestrator itself performs: repo hooks
# (husky…) expect the node_modules a fresh worktree doesn't have, gpg signing hangs
# headless, and an unset user.name fails commits outright.
GIT_CFG = ["-c", "core.hooksPath=/dev/null", "-c", "commit.gpgsign=false",
           "-c", "user.name=pervio-orchestrate", "-c", "user.email=pervio@localhost"]


def _git(cwd, *args, new_session=False, timeout=None):
    """Run git -C cwd with the hook-free config. Returns (rc, stdout, stderr).
    Local git ops are fast and left un-timed; pass timeout for a NETWORK op (a
    submodule fetch can hang on a stalled remote and, with #4's identity check, wedge
    the run's schedule until reboot). On timeout git is SIGKILLed and rc 124 is
    returned — enough to fail the step and let the schedule recover; a stray fetch
    grandchild may briefly linger, but the network op it's on is what was stuck."""
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(["git"] + GIT_CFG + ["-C", cwd] + list(args),
                           stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, capture_output=True, text=True,
                           start_new_session=new_session, timeout=timeout)
        return p.returncode, (p.stdout or "").strip(), (p.stderr or "").strip()
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return 124, "", "git timed out (>%ss)" % int(timeout)
    except OSError as e:
        return 127, "", str(e)


class _LandingLock(object):
    """One workflow at a time puts its result back on a repository's branch.

    Two workflows that finish together on the same repository used to do it at
    the same moment: both saw the checkout where they had left it, both ran
    `git merge --ff-only`, and git — which does not make "rewrite the index and
    the files, then move the branch" one step — let one win the branch and the
    other win the index. The person was left with a staged rename they never
    made and stray untracked files (the suite's S7 caught it about one run in
    four). Holding this lock across "is the checkout still where we started?"
    and the merge makes the second workflow see that the first has moved the
    branch, and keep its results on its own branch instead.

    A flock on a file in the repository's own git directory, so it follows the
    repository, binds every process whatever its HOME, costs the working tree
    nothing, and is let go by the kernel if the holder dies. `held` is False
    when another workflow kept it for the whole wait — then nothing is merged;
    when the file cannot be opened at all the merge runs as it always did."""

    def __init__(self, top, wait=60.0):
        self.top, self.wait, self.f, self.held = top, wait, None, True

    def __enter__(self):
        import fcntl
        rc, common, _ = _git(self.top, "rev-parse", "--git-common-dir")
        if rc != 0 or not common:
            return self
        if not os.path.isabs(common):
            common = os.path.join(self.top, common)
        try:
            self.f = open(os.path.join(common, "pervio-landing"), "a")
        except OSError:
            return self
        deadline = time.time() + self.wait
        while True:
            try:
                fcntl.flock(self.f, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
                return self
            except OSError:
                if time.time() >= deadline:
                    self.held = False
                    return self
                time.sleep(0.05)

    def __exit__(self, *exc):
        if self.f is not None:
            try:
                import fcntl
                fcntl.flock(self.f, fcntl.LOCK_UN)
            except OSError:
                pass
            self.f.close()
        return False


def _is_reader(s):
    """Fail-CLOSED write classification: only plan / read-only sandbox with no
    verify gate count as readers; anything else (default/auto/acceptEdits/bypass,
    missing or typo'd codex sandbox, any verify — it runs unsandboxed) is a writer."""
    # A fan-out step runs no agent turn of its OWN — its children write only in
    # ephemeral worktrees (discarded), never the main repo — so it's a reader, and
    # must never trigger P3 main-repo isolation for itself.
    if s.get("fanout"):
        return True
    if s.get("verify"):
        return False
    if s.get("backend", "claude") == "codex":
        return s.get("sandbox") == "read-only"
    return s.get("perm") == "plan"


def _step_refs(s):
    """All refs a step consumes: from its prompt, its fanout.over source (the
    fan-out step's real input, not carried in the prompt) AND its stdin
    template (a command step's real input, same story)."""
    refs = list(_prompt_refs(s.get("prompt", "")))
    fo = s.get("fanout")
    srcs = [fo.get("over", "")] if isinstance(fo, dict) else []
    if isinstance(s.get("stdin"), str):
        srcs.append(s["stdin"])
    for src in srcs:
        for r in _prompt_refs(src):
            if r not in refs:
                refs.append(r)
    return refs


def _dep_edges(steps):
    """stepId -> set of ids it waits on: dependsOn ∪ {{id.output}} refs (to real
    ids). Refs are ordering edges — the phone already draws them as such, and a
    consumer racing its producer would otherwise substitute ""."""
    ids = {s["id"] for s in steps}
    return {s["id"]: ({d for d in (s.get("dependsOn") or []) if d in ids}
                      | {r for r in _step_refs(s) if r in ids})
            for s in steps}


def _ancestors(dep):
    """Transitive closure of _dep_edges (cycle-tolerant)."""
    anc = {}

    def walk(x, seen):
        if x in anc:
            return anc[x]
        out = set()
        for d in dep.get(x, ()):
            if d in seen:
                continue
            out.add(d)
            out |= walk(d, seen | {d})
        anc[x] = out
        return out

    for x in dep:
        walk(x, {x})
    return anc


def _plan_isolation(spec, steps):
    """Decide isolation up front. Returns None for SHARED (exact legacy behavior:
    not a git repo, unborn HEAD, or no two write steps can run concurrently), else
    {top, base, dep, in_repo: id->bool, prefix: id->subdir-under-top}."""
    droot = os.path.realpath(_expand_dir(spec.get("dir", "~")))
    rc, top, _ = _git(droot, "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel")
    if rc != 0 or not top:
        return None
    rc, base, _ = _git(droot, "rev-parse", "HEAD")
    if rc != 0 or not base:
        return None
    top = os.path.realpath(top)
    in_repo, prefix = {}, {}
    for s in steps:
        sdir = os.path.realpath(_expand_dir(s.get("dir", spec.get("dir", "~"))))
        inside = sdir == top or sdir.startswith(top + os.sep)
        in_repo[s["id"]] = inside
        if inside:
            prefix[s["id"]] = os.path.relpath(sdir, top)
    writers = [s["id"] for s in steps if in_repo[s["id"]] and not _is_reader(s)]
    if len(writers) < 2:
        return None
    dep = _dep_edges(steps)
    anc = _ancestors(dep)
    if not any(a not in anc.get(b, ()) and b not in anc.get(a, ())
               for i, a in enumerate(writers) for b in writers[i + 1:]):
        return None
    return {"top": top, "base": base, "dep": dep, "in_repo": in_repo, "prefix": prefix}


# ---------- dynamic fan-out (P4-next): one child agent turn per list item ----------

def _fanout_split(strategy, text):
    """Split an upstream list into per-item units. Robust against real LLM output
    (preamble/sign-off lines, markdown fences). Returns a list of item strings."""
    text = text or ""
    if strategy == "lines":
        return [l for l in text.splitlines() if l.strip()]
    if strategy == "json":
        s = re.sub(r"(?s)^```[a-zA-Z]*\s*|\s*```$", "", text.strip()).strip()   # strip fences
        try:
            v = json.loads(s)
        except ValueError:
            m = re.search(r"(?s)\[.*\]", s)                                       # bracket fallback
            v = json.loads(m.group(0)) if m else None
        if not isinstance(v, list):
            raise ValueError("json split did not yield a list")
        return [x if isinstance(x, str) else json.dumps(x, ensure_ascii=False) for x in v]
    # default: numbered — split BEFORE each "N." / "N)" line; drop any preamble /
    # sign-off chunk that doesn't itself start with a number, and strip the number.
    items = []
    for part in re.split(r"(?m)^(?=\s*\d+[.)])", text):
        part = part.strip()
        if re.match(r"^\d+[.)]", part):
            items.append(re.sub(r"^\s*\d+[.)]\s*", "", part).strip())
    return items


def _run_fanout(s, prompt_tmpl, snap, snap_json, led, lock, save, wf, sid, backend,
                default_dir):
    """Execute a fan-out step: split the upstream list (or take a structured
    array verbatim), run ONE child agent turn per item (reusing the P4
    ephemeral-worktree child core, but UNGATED — we cap N here, not via the
    dispatch budget/width ledger), collect. Returns (ok, output, err, jarr):
    jarr is the step's structured value — the ok children's parsed values
    (with outputSchema) or their texts (without), in item order. The step is
    done if >=1 child succeeds (>= minSuccessRate); its output is the joined
    per-item results with a live "N/M done" summary the phone shows verbatim."""
    fo = s["fanout"]
    repo_top = os.path.realpath(_expand_dir(s.get("dir", default_dir)))
    rc, top, _ = _git(repo_top, "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel")
    if rc != 0 or not top:
        # Not a git repo — fan out anyway: run_child's no-repo mode runs each
        # child contained in a throwaway scratch dir instead of a worktree (no
        # branches to audit in this mode; delete's sweep already tolerates it).
        top = None
    else:
        top = os.path.realpath(top)

    over = fo.get("over", "") or ""
    mj = JSON_REF_RE.search(over)
    if mj:
        # Structured source: the producer's parsed array IS the item list —
        # no split heuristics, no numbered-list contract to keep a model honest to.
        src_v = snap_json.get(mj.group(1))
        if not isinstance(src_v, list):
            return False, "", ("fanout source %r has no structured array "
                               "(producer failed or produced a non-array)" % over), None
        items = list(src_v)
    else:
        m = REF_RE.search(over)
        src = snap.get(m.group(1), "") if m else ""
        if not src.strip():
            return False, "", "fanout source %r is empty or not done" % over, None
        # A truncated capture is a PARTIAL list. Fanning out over it silently
        # drops every item past the cut (2026-09-01: 7 of the digest's 19 score
        # shards, 92 papers) — refuse loudly instead; raising the cap or
        # shrinking the producer is the caller's call.
        if CMD_TRUNC_MARK in src[-64:]:
            return False, "", ("fanout source %r is TRUNCATED (producer output "
                               "exceeded PERVIO_CMD_MAX_OUTPUT=%d) — refusing to "
                               "fan out over a partial list" % (over, CMD_MAX_OUTPUT)), None
        try:
            items = _fanout_split(fo.get("split", "numbered"), src)
        except (ValueError, TypeError) as e:
            return False, "", "fanout split failed: %s" % e, None
    if not items:
        led["fanoutTotal"] = 0
        return False, "", "fanout produced 0 items", None

    total_found = len(items)
    max_items = int(fo.get("max", 50) or 50)
    truncated = max(0, total_found - max_items)
    items = items[:max_items]
    n = len(items)
    led["fanoutTotal"] = n
    if truncated:
        led["fanoutTruncated"] = truncated

    # {{item}} carries the whole item (objects as canonical JSON); {{item.key}}
    # reaches into an OBJECT item's fields so a prompt can say {{item.title}}
    # instead of teaching the model to re-parse the blob it was handed.
    items_text = [x if isinstance(x, str) else _json_text(x) for x in items]
    as_var = fo.get("as", "item")
    item_re = re.compile(r"\{\{\s*" + re.escape(as_var) + r"\s*\}\}")
    key_re = re.compile(r"\{\{\s*" + re.escape(as_var) + r"\.([A-Za-z0-9_-]+)\s*\}\}")
    child_schema = s.get("outputSchema")
    child_model = resolve_model(s.get("model"))
    # children run at the step's own tier, clamped (never bypass/danger).
    requested = s.get("sandbox", "read-only") if backend == "codex" else s.get("perm", "plan")
    try:
        eff = clamp_tier(backend, requested, backend, requested)
    except DispatchError as de:
        return False, "", "fanout tier: %s" % de.detail, None
    child_env = {
        "PERVIO_WF_ID": wf, "PERVIO_STEP_ID": sid,
        "PERVIO_REPO_TOP": top or "",   # empty ⇒ a grandchild dispatch fails closed
        "PERVIO_PARENT_BACKEND": backend, "PERVIO_PARENT_TIER": eff,
        "PERVIO_DISPATCH_DEPTH": "0",
    }
    base = os.path.join(WF, wf, "dispatch", "fanout", sid)
    results = [None] * n                     # (ok, text, err) per item
    parallel = max(1, min(int(fo.get("maxParallel", 4) or 4), MAX_PARALLEL))
    # Per-child wall clock: a spec may raise it for deep-read jobs (fanout.timeoutSec),
    # clamped to [60, 900] — the floor stops a typo'd "5" from insta-killing every
    # child, the ceiling bounds a stuck N×∞ pile-up. The PERVIO_CHILD_TIMEOUT env
    # default is operator-owned and stays unclamped.
    try:
        child_timeout = float(fo.get("timeoutSec") or 0)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        child_timeout = 0.0
    child_timeout = min(max(child_timeout, 60.0), 900.0) if child_timeout > 0 else CHILD_TIMEOUT
    led["fanoutTimeout"] = int(child_timeout)

    def one(i):
        def key_sub(mm):
            x = items[i]
            if isinstance(x, dict):
                vv = x.get(mm.group(1))
                if vv is None:
                    return ""
                return vv if isinstance(vv, str) else _json_text(vv)
            return ""                          # {{item.key}} on a non-object item
        item_prompt = key_re.sub(key_sub, prompt_tmpl)
        item_prompt = item_re.sub(lambda _m: items_text[i], item_prompt)
        b64 = base64.b64encode(item_prompt.encode("utf-8")).decode("ascii")
        cdir = os.path.join(base, str(i))
        branch = None if top is None else "pervio/wf-%s/dispatch/fanout/%s/%d" % (wf, sid, i)
        try:
            ok, text, cost, err = run_child(
                b64, backend, eff, top, cdir, branch, 0, child_timeout, CHAT,
                child_model, s.get("effort"), None, bool(s.get("allowNetwork")),
                extra_env=child_env, on_spawn=_live_register, on_reap=_live_unregister)
            if not ok or child_schema is None:
                return (ok, text, err, text if ok else None)
            val, verr = _parse_and_validate(child_schema, text)
            if not verr:
                return (True, text, "", val)
            # One corrective re-run, in a SIBLING dir (a worktree can't be
            # re-added in place); the final answer is then moved onto the
            # canonical out.jsonl so downstream child_text readers keep their
            # one path, with attempt 1 archived beside it for autopsies.
            fix_b64 = base64.b64encode(
                (item_prompt + SCHEMA_FIX_NOTE % (verr, (text or "")[:1500]))
                .encode("utf-8")).decode("ascii")
            ok2, text2, cost2, err2 = run_child(
                fix_b64, backend, eff, top, cdir + "-r2",
                (branch + "-r2") if branch else None, 0, child_timeout, CHAT,
                child_model, s.get("effort"), None, bool(s.get("allowNetwork")),
                extra_env=child_env, on_spawn=_live_register, on_reap=_live_unregister)
            try:
                os.replace(os.path.join(cdir, "out.jsonl"),
                           os.path.join(cdir, "out.jsonl.badjson1"))
                os.replace(os.path.join(cdir + "-r2", "out.jsonl"),
                           os.path.join(cdir, "out.jsonl"))
            except OSError:
                pass
            if ok2:
                val, verr = _parse_and_validate(child_schema, text2)
                if not verr:
                    return (True, text2, "", val)
                return (False, text2, "输出不符合契约(schema): %s" % verr, None)
            return (False, "", "输出不符合契约(schema): %s; 纠错重试也失败: %s"
                    % (verr, err2), None)
        except DispatchError as de:
            return (False, "", de.code, None)
        except Exception as e:                # a child must never crash the whole fan-out
            return (False, "", str(e)[:120], None)

    def render(done_count, fail_count):
        parts = []
        for i, r in enumerate(results):
            if r is None:
                continue
            ok, text, err = r[0], r[1], r[2]
            head = "[%d] %s" % (i + 1, "OK" if ok else ("FAIL: " + (err or "")))
            body = (text or "")[:4096]
            if text and len(text) > 4096:
                body += "\n…[truncated]"
            parts.append(head + "\n" + body)
        summary = "%d/%d done (%d failed)" % (done_count, n, fail_count)
        if truncated:
            summary += " · %d dropped by max" % truncated
        return summary + "\n\n" + "\n---\n".join(parts)

    done_count = fail_count = 0
    with concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=parallel) as ex:
        futs = {ex.submit(one, i): i for i in range(n)}
        for f in concurrent.futures.as_completed(futs):
            i = futs[f]
            results[i] = f.result()
            done_count += 1
            if not results[i][0]:
                fail_count += 1
            with lock:                        # live progress the phone shows verbatim
                led["fanoutOk"] = done_count - fail_count   # live counts too, so the
                led["fanoutFail"] = fail_count              # step row's chip can tick up
                led["output"] = render(done_count, fail_count)
                save()

    n_ok = sum(1 for r in results if r and r[0])
    led["fanoutOk"] = n_ok
    led["fanoutFail"] = n - n_ok
    min_rate = float(fo.get("minSuccessRate", 0.0) or 0.0)
    # tolerateAllFail: a scheduled pipeline can prefer a DEGRADED downstream step
    # (which sees the FAIL lines and redoes the work itself) over a skipped one —
    # with it, the step is done no matter how many children failed, and it
    # overrides minSuccessRate (the two knobs contradict; tolerance wins).
    ok = bool(fo.get("tolerateAllFail")) or (n_ok >= 1 and (n_ok / n) >= min_rate)
    output = render(n_ok, n - n_ok)
    err = "" if ok else ("all %d children failed" % n if n_ok == 0
                         else "%d/%d below minSuccessRate %s" % (n_ok, n, min_rate))
    # The step's structured value: ok children's parsed values (schema) or texts
    # (no schema), in ITEM order — failures simply aren't in it, so a downstream
    # consumer never has to strip FAIL markers again.
    jarr = [r[3] for r in results if r and r[0] and r[3] is not None]
    return ok, output, err, jarr


def _die(msg, code=2):
    sys.stderr.write("pervio-orchestrate: %s\n" % msg)
    sys.exit(code)


def _parse(argv, flags):
    out = {}
    i = 0
    while i < len(argv):
        a = argv[i]
        if a.startswith("--") and a[2:] in flags:
            if i + 1 >= len(argv):
                _die("missing value for %s" % a)
            out[a[2:]] = argv[i + 1]
            i += 2
        else:
            _die("unknown/malformed arg: %s" % a)
    return out


def _wf_dir(wf_id):
    if not wf_id or not VALID_ID.match(wf_id):
        _die("bad workflow id")
    return os.path.join(WF, wf_id)


def _read_json(path, default=None):
    """Missing file → default: a legitimate 'nothing written yet'. But a file that
    EXISTS and fails to read/parse is a real fault (disk error, external truncation),
    not emptiness — surface it on stderr (runner.err / the tick log) instead of
    silently returning {} that looks identical to an empty ledger. Still returns
    default so a caller mid-run doesn't crash on one bad read."""
    try:
        with open(path, "r", errors="replace") as f:
            return json.load(f)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return {} if default is None else default
    except (OSError, ValueError) as e:
        sys.stderr.write("pervio-orchestrate: corrupt/unreadable %s: %s\n"
                         % (path, str(e)[:200]))
        return {} if default is None else default


def _write_atomic(path, obj):
    # Per-writer tmp name: two processes writing the same path (a tick and a phone
    # verb touching the same schedule.json) must not share one ".tmp" and interleave
    # into a half-written file before os.replace.
    tmp = "%s.tmp.%d" % (path, os.getpid())
    with open(tmp, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        json.dump(obj, f, ensure_ascii=False)
    os.replace(tmp, path)


def _expand_dir(d):
    d = d or "~"
    if d == "~":
        return HOME
    if d.startswith("~/"):
        return os.path.join(HOME, d[2:])
    return d


# ---------- start ----------

def _dir_ok(d):
    """A spec/step `dir` may be absent (inherit), '~'-relative, or absolute — all the
    user's explicit choice, and no worse than what a command step could `cd` to. What
    we refuse is a '..' component: the path-traversal shape ('~/../.ssh') that lets an
    IMPORTED workflow silently point steps outside where the importer thinks it runs."""
    if d is None:
        return True
    if not isinstance(d, str) or not d.strip():
        return False
    return ".." not in d.replace("\\", "/").split("/")


def _validate_spec(spec):
    """Raise ValueError on a malformed spec (shared by start and schedule tick —
    tick must record the error instead of dying)."""
    if isinstance(spec, dict) and spec.get("kind") == "playbook":
        # A playbook wrapper ({"kind":"playbook","body":…}) is conversation
        # methodology, not a runnable spec. EVERY launch door funnels through
        # here — start, the whole schedule family (add/update/fire/tick), and
        # rerun's spec round trip — so this one gate refuses them all with the
        # same words the app and MCP surface verbatim.
        raise ValueError("playbook cannot be started as a workflow — "
                         "load it into a conversation")
    steps = spec.get("steps")
    if not isinstance(steps, list) or not steps:
        raise ValueError("spec needs a non-empty steps[]")
    if not _dir_ok(spec.get("dir")):
        raise ValueError("spec dir %r is invalid (no '..' traversal)" % spec.get("dir"))
    for i, s in enumerate(steps):
        if not isinstance(s, dict) or not s.get("id") or not VALID_ID.match(str(s.get("id"))):
            raise ValueError("step %d needs a valid id" % i)
        if str(s.get("id")) in RESERVED_IDS:
            raise ValueError("step id %r is reserved" % s.get("id"))
        if not _dir_ok(s.get("dir")):
            raise ValueError("step %s dir %r is invalid (no '..' traversal)"
                             % (s.get("id"), s.get("dir")))
        cmdline = s.get("command")
        if cmdline is not None and not (isinstance(cmdline, str) and cmdline.strip()):
            raise ValueError("step %s command must be a non-empty string" % s.get("id"))
        # dependsOn was taken on faith: a non-list (an imported or hand-edited spec)
        # raised inside the runner's DAG loop, killed the detached runner mid-flight
        # and left ledger.status "running" forever; a bare string iterated into
        # characters, i.e. a dependency on steps that don't exist. It is the ORDER of
        # the whole run — check the shape here, at the door.
        dep = s.get("dependsOn")
        if dep is not None:
            if not isinstance(dep, list):
                raise ValueError("step %s dependsOn must be a list of step ids, got %s"
                                 % (s.get("id"), type(dep).__name__))
            for d in dep:
                if not isinstance(d, str) or not d.strip():
                    raise ValueError("step %s dependsOn entries must be non-empty step ids"
                                     % s.get("id"))
        fo = s.get("fanout")
        if fo is not None:
            if cmdline:
                raise ValueError("step %s can't be both a command and a fan-out" % s.get("id"))
            if not isinstance(fo, dict) or not (fo.get("over") or "").strip():
                raise ValueError("step %s fanout needs an 'over' source" % s.get("id"))
            if fo.get("split") not in (None, "numbered", "lines", "json"):
                raise ValueError("step %s fanout split must be numbered|lines|json" % s.get("id"))
            if fo.get("tolerateAllFail") not in (None, True, False):
                raise ValueError("step %s fanout tolerateAllFail must be a boolean" % s.get("id"))
            if fo.get("max") is not None:
                fmax = fo["max"]
                # Require a real int. bool is an int subclass (reject True/False);
                # float/str/None are out — and NOT calling int() means JSON Infinity
                # (→ float('inf')) can't slip through as an uncaught OverflowError, and
                # 1.9 isn't silently truncated to 1.
                if isinstance(fmax, bool) or not isinstance(fmax, int):
                    raise ValueError("step %s fanout max must be an integer" % s.get("id"))
                if fmax < 1 or fmax > FANOUT_MAX_CAP:
                    raise ValueError("step %s fanout max must be 1..%d"
                                     % (s.get("id"), FANOUT_MAX_CAP))
        if s.get("optional") not in (None, True, False):
            raise ValueError("step %s optional must be a boolean" % s.get("id"))
        rt = s.get("retry")
        if rt is not None:
            # A real small int: bool is an int subclass (True is not "1 retry"),
            # and >5 is a cost bug typed as a preference.
            if isinstance(rt, bool) or not isinstance(rt, int) or rt < 0 or rt > 5:
                raise ValueError("step %s retry must be an integer 0..5" % s.get("id"))
        sch = s.get("outputSchema")
        if sch is not None:
            # The schema constrains an AGENT's answer; a command's stdout is the
            # author's own program — validating it would only mask their bug.
            if s.get("command"):
                raise ValueError("step %s: outputSchema applies to agent steps, "
                                 "not commands" % s.get("id"))
            if not isinstance(sch, dict):
                raise ValueError("step %s outputSchema must be an object "
                                 "(a JSON-Schema subset)" % s.get("id"))
        sti = s.get("stdin")
        if sti is not None:
            # stdin is the data lane INTO a command (the command line stays a
            # fixed string). An agent step's input is its prompt; a stdin there
            # is a confusion worth refusing loudly.
            if not s.get("command"):
                raise ValueError("step %s: stdin only applies to command steps"
                                 % s.get("id"))
            if not isinstance(sti, str):
                raise ValueError("step %s stdin must be a string template"
                                 % s.get("id"))
        if fo is not None and JSON_REF_RE.search((fo.get("over") or "")) \
                and fo.get("split") is not None:
            # A structured source IS the item list; a split strategy on top can
            # only contradict it.
            raise ValueError("step %s: fanout over {{id.json}} is already "
                             "item-shaped — remove the split" % s.get("id"))
        pub = s.get("publish")
        if pub is False:
            # A hand-written `false` means "not published" — tolerate it as
            # absent instead of erroring (the app's encoder omits the key, but
            # JSON edited by hand shouldn't be punished for being explicit).
            pub = None
            s.pop("publish", None)
        if pub is not None and pub is not True:
            # publish marks this step's output as the workflow's publication.
            # Exactly true (default archive dir) or a non-empty dir string —
            # anything else (1, {}) is a typo'd spec, not a preference.
            if not (isinstance(pub, str) and pub.strip()):
                raise ValueError("step %s publish must be true or a directory string"
                                 % s.get("id"))
            if not _dir_ok(pub):
                raise ValueError("step %s publish dir %r is invalid (no '..' traversal)"
                                 % (s.get("id"), pub))
        if pub is not None and s.get("fanout") is not None:
            # A fan-out's aggregate output carries FAIL markers and progress
            # headers — machinery, not a publication. Publish the downstream
            # step that synthesizes it instead. (Silently no-op'ing here was
            # worse: the card would just never appear.)
            raise ValueError("step %s can't publish a fan-out's aggregate — "
                             "publish the downstream synthesis step" % s.get("id"))
    ids = [str(s["id"]) for s in steps]
    if len(set(ids)) != len(ids):
        # Duplicates used to silently collapse in by_id; under worktree isolation
        # they'd race one branch/worktree path mid-run — reject up front.
        raise ValueError("duplicate step ids")
    # Every {{id.output}} ref (prompt or fanout.over) must name a real step. A ref to
    # nothing used to substitute "" at runtime: the consumer quietly worked from an
    # empty input and the run still reported `done` — a wrong answer nobody sees.
    # Refuse the START instead, before a single turn is paid for.
    idset = set(ids)
    for s in steps:
        for r in _step_refs(s):
            if r not in idset:
                raise ValueError("step %s references {{%s.output}} — no step with id %r"
                                 % (s.get("id"), r, r))
        # Same rule for the timing edges: _dep_edges keeps only real ids, so a
        # dangling dependsOn would silently become "no dependency at all" — the step
        # would run early against an unfinished producer. Say so at the door instead.
        for d in (s.get("dependsOn") or []):
            if d not in idset:
                raise ValueError("step %s dependsOn %r — no step with that id"
                                 % (s.get("id"), d))
    # {{id.json}} may only name a step that actually produces a structured
    # value: a fan-out (ok-children array) or an agent step with outputSchema.
    # Referencing anything else would substitute "" at runtime — the silent
    # empty-input class this validator exists to kill.
    structured_ids = {str(s["id"]) for s in steps
                      if s.get("fanout") is not None
                      or (s.get("outputSchema") is not None and not s.get("command"))}
    for s in steps:
        texts = [s.get("prompt", ""), s.get("stdin") or ""]
        if isinstance(s.get("fanout"), dict):
            texts.append(s["fanout"].get("over", "") or "")
        for txt in texts:
            for r in JSON_REF_RE.findall(txt):
                if r in idset and r not in structured_ids:
                    raise ValueError(
                        "step %s references {{%s.json}} but step %r has no "
                        "structured output (needs fanout or outputSchema)"
                        % (s.get("id"), r, r))
    # ...and the edges those refs create must not form a CYCLE. Making refs into
    # ordering edges (so a consumer can't race its producer) turned a self- or
    # mutually-referencing spec from "completes with an empty substitution" into
    # "the scheduler waits forever and the run fails" — a regression for anyone
    # who had such a spec working. Reject it at the door with the cycle spelled
    # out, so nobody pays for a run that cannot finish.
    dep = _dep_edges(steps)
    state = {}                       # id -> 1 visiting, 2 done
    stack = []

    def walk(node):
        if state.get(node) == 2:
            return
        if state.get(node) == 1:
            cyc = stack[stack.index(node):] + [node]
            raise ValueError("dependency cycle: " + " → ".join(cyc)
                             + " (a {{id.output}} reference is an ordering edge)")
        state[node] = 1
        stack.append(node)
        for nxt in sorted(dep.get(node) or ()):
            walk(nxt)
        stack.pop()
        state[node] = 2

    for sid_ in ids:
        walk(sid_)
    # A fan-out `as` var must not collide with a real step id (would shadow a ref).
    for s in steps:
        as_var = (s.get("fanout") or {}).get("as", "item")
        if as_var in set(ids):
            raise ValueError("step %s fanout 'as' %r collides with a step id" % (s.get("id"), as_var))
    # The publication is singular — one home card, one back-issue reader per
    # workflow — so at most one step may carry publish.
    if sum(1 for s in steps if s.get("publish") is not None) > 1:
        raise ValueError("only one step may publish")


def _params_from_b64(raw):
    """--params-b64 payload: a JSON object of name->value. Mechanical layer only —
    names must fit the placeholder grammar, values must be str/int/bool (anything
    else is rejected outright; there is no coercion). Declaration-level checks
    (required/default/type-vs-declaration) belong to pervio-mcp, which holds the
    template wrapper; a direct CLI caller owns its own values."""
    try:
        obj = json.loads(base64.b64decode(raw).decode("utf-8"))
    except Exception as e:
        raise ValueError("bad params: %s" % e)
    if not isinstance(obj, dict):
        raise ValueError("params must be a JSON object of name->value")
    for k, v in obj.items():
        if not isinstance(k, str) or not PARAM_NAME.match(k):
            raise ValueError("param name %r is invalid (^[a-z][a-z0-9_]{0,23}$)" % (k,))
        # bool first: it subclasses int, and bools ARE accepted (rendered true/false).
        if not isinstance(v, (bool, int, str)):
            raise ValueError("param %r value must be a string/integer/boolean, got %s"
                             % (k, type(v).__name__))
        # Second-order injection gate: a value carrying a RUNTIME placeholder
        # ({{step.output}} / {{item}}) would land verbatim in the prompt and be
        # expanded by the later ref pass with that step's real output — a param
        # must stay data all the way down. Same fail-closed posture as the
        # {{param. leftover probe.
        if isinstance(v, str) and (ANY_REF_RE.search(v) or "{{item}}" in v):
            raise ValueError("param %r value must not contain runtime placeholders "
                             "({{step.output}} / {{step.json}} / {{item}})" % (k,))
    return obj


def _param_text(v):
    """A param value as prompt/dir text: strings verbatim, ints as digits,
    bools as JSON literals (true/false)."""
    if isinstance(v, bool):
        return "true" if v else "false"
    return v if isinstance(v, str) else str(v)


def _param_leftover(x):
    """True if any string ANYWHERE in the spec (keys included) still carries a
    {{param. prefix — the fail-closed probe behind the whole-start rejection."""
    if isinstance(x, str):
        return PARAM_LEFTOVER.search(x) is not None
    if isinstance(x, list):
        return any(_param_leftover(v) for v in x)
    if isinstance(x, dict):
        return any(_param_leftover(k) or _param_leftover(v) for k, v in x.items())
    return False


def _apply_params(spec, params):
    """F2 mechanical substitution: {{param.<name>}} -> value, in each step's
    `prompt`, its `stdin` DATA lane, and the top-level spec `dir` ONLY — params
    never reach a shell (command/verify are fixed strings, same trust level as
    before; stdin is piped bytes, not shell — 2026-09-02, so a parameterized
    command step reads its knobs off stdin instead of being unbuildable). Single pass:
    a substituted VALUE that itself contains "{{param." is NOT re-expanded — the
    leftover probe below rejects the start instead (fail closed). Any leftover
    anywhere (a placeholder in a command/verify/step-dir, an unknown name, a
    malformed name) rejects the whole start."""
    if not isinstance(spec, dict):
        return                      # _validate_spec owns the shape complaint

    def sub(text):
        return PARAM_RE.sub(
            lambda m: _param_text(params[m.group(1)]) if m.group(1) in params
            else m.group(0), text)

    if isinstance(spec.get("dir"), str):
        spec["dir"] = sub(spec["dir"])
    steps = spec.get("steps")
    for s in (steps if isinstance(steps, list) else []):
        if isinstance(s, dict) and isinstance(s.get("prompt"), str):
            s["prompt"] = sub(s["prompt"])
        if isinstance(s, dict) and isinstance(s.get("stdin"), str):
            s["stdin"] = sub(s["stdin"])
    if _param_leftover(spec):
        raise ValueError("unresolved {{param.*}} placeholder remains after "
                         "substitution — params only substitute into prompts, "
                         "dir and stdin")


def _spec_to_start(o, verb):
    """The spec a start would run, from --spec-b64 [--params-b64]: decoded,
    parameters filled in, validated. One function for `start` and `resolve`, so
    what `resolve` prints is what `start` runs."""
    if "spec-b64" not in o:
        _die("%s needs --spec-b64" % verb)
    try:
        spec = json.loads(base64.b64decode(o["spec-b64"]).decode("utf-8"))
    except Exception as e:
        _die("bad spec: %s" % e)
    if "params-b64" in o:
        # Substitution runs BEFORE _validate_spec, so the substituted dir goes
        # through the exact same _dir_ok gate as a literal one below.
        try:
            _apply_params(spec, _params_from_b64(o["params-b64"]))
        except ValueError as e:
            _die(str(e))
    try:
        _validate_spec(spec)
    except ValueError as e:
        _die(str(e))
    return spec


def cmd_resolve(argv):
    """What `start` would run for the same --spec-b64 [--params-b64], printed as
    JSON and NOT started: parameters filled in, the spec validated. pervio-mcp
    asks this before a start made from a conversation, so the check it holds
    the workflow to — and the sheet the phone shows — are about the workflow as
    it would really run (security review 2026-11-22, #1 and #2)."""
    o = _parse(argv, {"spec-b64", "params-b64"})
    sys.stdout.write(json.dumps(_spec_to_start(o, "resolve"), ensure_ascii=False) + "\n")


def cmd_start(argv):
    o = _parse(argv, {"spec-b64", "params-b64", "resume-from", "device", "origin"})
    spec = _spec_to_start(o, "start")
    try:
        # --device / --origin: the phone (its push device id) and the server entry
        # on it that started this workflow — the completion push goes to that
        # phone only, as it already does for a run (谁发的推给谁, 2026-10-18).
        who = {k: o[k] for k in ("device", "origin") if o.get(k)}
        wf_id = _start_wf(spec, extra=who or None, resume_from=o.get("resume-from"))
    except ValueError as e:
        _die(str(e))
    sys.stdout.write(json.dumps({"wfId": wf_id}) + "\n")


def _resume_cache_ids(spec, old_rd):
    """Which steps of `spec` can reuse the old run's results outright: the old
    run finished them, the step's canonical JSON is unchanged, and every
    dependency is itself cached — the first edited step breaks the chain for
    everything downstream of it. Returns (cached_ids, old_ledger_steps_by_id).

    Worktree-isolated workflows are refused: a cached write step's commits
    live on branches of the OLD run — a new integration branch can't
    resurrect that git state honestly, and pretending otherwise would merge
    phantom work."""
    old_spec = _read_json(os.path.join(old_rd, "spec.json"))
    old_led = _read_json(os.path.join(old_rd, "ledger.json"))
    if not old_spec.get("steps") or not old_led.get("steps"):
        raise ValueError("resume: old run has no spec/ledger to reuse")
    steps = spec.get("steps") or []
    if _plan_isolation(spec, steps):
        raise ValueError("resume is not supported for worktree-isolated workflows "
                         "(parallel write steps) — start it fresh")
    # The OLD run's isolation matters too (2026-08-22 audit, P1): its write
    # steps' commits live on ITS branches. If they never merged back, caching
    # those steps as 'done' would build the new run on a tree that LACKS their
    # work — a silent wrong result. Merged runs are safe: the work is already
    # in the user's branch. Checking only the new spec was the exact hole.
    old_git = old_led.get("git") or {}
    if old_git and not old_git.get("merged"):
        raise ValueError("resume: the old run used worktree isolation and its "
                         "work never merged back (%s) — its steps can't be "
                         "reused honestly; start fresh"
                         % (old_git.get("branch") or "results on its branches"))
    old_led_by = {st.get("id"): st for st in old_led.get("steps", [])}
    if (old_spec.get("dir", "~") != spec.get("dir", "~")
            or old_spec.get("setup") != spec.get("setup")):
        return set(), old_led_by      # different ground under every step: no reuse
    old_by = {s.get("id"): s for s in old_spec.get("steps", [])}

    def canon(x):
        return json.dumps(x, sort_keys=True, ensure_ascii=False)

    dep = _dep_edges(steps)
    cached = set()
    changed = True
    while changed:                    # steps[] isn't topo-sorted; fixpoint is
        changed = False
        for s in steps:
            sid = s["id"]
            if sid in cached:
                continue
            osd, ol = old_by.get(sid), old_led_by.get(sid)
            if not osd or not ol or ol.get("status") != "done":
                continue
            if canon(osd) != canon(s):
                continue
            if any(d not in cached for d in dep.get(sid, ())):
                continue
            # A structured step's cache is its json artifact — a downstream
            # {{id.json}} of a cached step with no artifact would substitute ""
            # and quietly feed empty input (2026-08-22 audit, P2). No artifact,
            # no cache: the step (and its dependents) re-run instead.
            if (s.get("fanout") is not None
                    or (s.get("outputSchema") is not None and not s.get("command"))):
                if not os.path.exists(os.path.join(old_rd, "json", "%s.json" % sid)):
                    continue
            cached.add(sid)
            changed = True
    return cached, old_led_by


def _start_wf(spec, extra=None, resume_from=None):
    """Create the run dir + ledger and spawn the detached runner. Returns the wfId.
    `extra` merges extra fields into the ledger root (e.g. scheduleId, so list and
    the schedule pruner can tell a schedule's runs apart). Spec must be validated.
    `resume_from` seeds unchanged, already-done steps from that prior run
    (ledger 'done'+cached, json artifacts and fan-out children copied over) so
    only the edited tail spends tokens. Raises ValueError when resuming is
    impossible; a plain start never does."""
    steps = spec.get("steps")
    cached, old_led_by, old_rd = set(), {}, None
    if resume_from:
        if not VALID_ID.match(resume_from or ""):
            raise ValueError("resume: bad run id %r" % resume_from)
        old_rd = os.path.join(WF, resume_from)
        if not os.path.isdir(old_rd):
            raise ValueError("resume: no run %s" % resume_from)
        cached, old_led_by = _resume_cache_ids(spec, old_rd)
    os.makedirs(WF, mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
    wf_id = uuid.uuid4().hex[:12]
    rd = _wf_dir(wf_id)
    os.makedirs(rd, mode=0o700)

    _write_atomic(os.path.join(rd, "spec.json"), spec)
    for sid in sorted(cached):
        # The artifacts a cached step's CONSUMERS read: its parsed value and its
        # fan-out children's out.jsonls (assemble-style commands walk those by
        # path). Worktrees are dead weight and stay behind; conversations belong
        # to the old run, so sessionIds are deliberately NOT carried over.
        sj = os.path.join(old_rd, "json", "%s.json" % sid)
        if os.path.exists(sj):
            os.makedirs(os.path.join(rd, "json"), mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
            shutil.copy2(sj, os.path.join(rd, "json", "%s.json" % sid))
        sf = os.path.join(old_rd, "dispatch", "fanout", sid)
        if os.path.isdir(sf):
            shutil.copytree(sf, os.path.join(rd, "dispatch", "fanout", sid),
                            ignore=shutil.ignore_patterns("wt"))
    ledger = {
        "wfId": wf_id,
        "name": spec.get("name", ""),
        "dir": spec.get("dir", "~"),
        "status": "running",
        "startedAt": int(time.time()),
        "steps": [dict({
            "id": s["id"],
            "backend": s.get("backend", "claude"),
            # Carried into the ledger (not just the spec) so the phone can draw the
            # graph even when ATTACHING to a workflow it didn't start: dependsOn =
            # timing (which row), inputs = data flow ({{id.output}} refs, the edges).
            "dependsOn": s.get("dependsOn") if isinstance(s.get("dependsOn"), list) else [],
            "inputs": _step_refs(s),
            "status": "pending",
            "output": "",
            "error": "",
            "bytes": 0,
        }, **({"command": True} if s.get("command") else {}),
           **({"optional": True} if s.get("optional") else {})) for s in steps],
    }
    for st in ledger["steps"]:
        if st["id"] in cached:
            ol = old_led_by.get(st["id"]) or {}
            st["status"] = "done"
            st["cached"] = True
            st["output"] = ol.get("output", "")
            st["bytes"] = ol.get("bytes", 0)
    if cached:
        ledger["resumedFrom"] = resume_from
    if extra:
        ledger.update(extra)
    _write_atomic(os.path.join(rd, "ledger.json"), ledger)
    with open(os.path.join(rd, "status"), "w") as f:
        f.write("running")

    # Detached runner: its own session (survives the SSH channel closing).
    #
    # Run the worker — and every agent it fans out — at a LOWER cpu/io priority, so a
    # big parallel workflow can never starve latency-critical processes on this box:
    # sshd and the frp client (nana) stay at the default priority and always win the
    # scheduler, so the SSH tunnel keeps answering heartbeats even under a heavy fan-out.
    # nice/ionice are inherited across fork+exec, so applying them to the runner covers
    # all its child agents. Degrade gracefully if either binary is missing.
    err_f = open(os.path.join(rd, "runner.err"), "wb")
    runner_cmd = [sys.executable, SELF, "run", "--wf", wf_id]
    if shutil.which("ionice"):
        runner_cmd = ["ionice", "-c2", "-n7"] + runner_cmd
    if shutil.which("nice"):
        runner_cmd = ["nice", "-n", "10"] + runner_cmd
    p = subprocess.Popen(
        runner_cmd,
        stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=err_f,
        start_new_session=True,
    )
    err_f.close()
    with open(os.path.join(rd, "pgid"), "w") as f:
        f.write(str(p.pid))
    # Record the leader's start token alongside the pgid so a later reboot that
    # recycles this pid can be told apart from our real still-running group
    # (see _pgid_identity_ok). start_new_session=True makes p.pid the pgid leader.
    tok = _proc_start(p.pid)
    if tok:
        with open(os.path.join(rd, "pgid.start"), "w") as f:
            f.write(tok)
    return wf_id


# ---------- run (detached worker) ----------

def _step_output(jsonl_path, backend):
    """Final text a step produced: last agent_message (codex) / result (claude) —
    plus the agent's session/thread id, recorded so deleting the workflow can also
    delete the conversations it created, plus the turn's token usage. Returns
    (text, err, session_id, tokens): tokens holds {tokensIn, tokensOut} (ints)
    when the backend reported usage — claude carries it on the result event,
    codex on turn.completed — and stays {} when the data isn't there."""
    text = ""
    err = ""
    agent_sid = ""
    tok = {}

    def take_usage(o):
        u = o.get("usage")
        if isinstance(u, dict):
            if isinstance(u.get("input_tokens"), int):
                tok["tokensIn"] = u["input_tokens"]
            if isinstance(u.get("output_tokens"), int):
                tok["tokensOut"] = u["output_tokens"]

    try:
        with open(jsonl_path, "r", errors="replace") as f:
            for line in f:
                line = line.strip()
                if not line:
                    continue
                try:
                    o = json.loads(line)
                except ValueError:
                    continue
                t = o.get("type")
                if backend == "codex":
                    if t == "thread.started" and o.get("thread_id"):
                        agent_sid = o["thread_id"]
                    if t == "item.completed":
                        it = o.get("item") or {}
                        if it.get("type") == "agent_message" and it.get("text"):
                            text = it["text"]
                    elif t in ("turn.failed", "error"):
                        err = (o.get("error") or {}).get("message") or o.get("message") or "failed"
                    elif t == "turn.completed":
                        take_usage(o)
                else:  # claude
                    if o.get("session_id"):
                        agent_sid = o["session_id"]
                    if t == "result":
                        if o.get("result"):
                            text = o["result"]
                        if o.get("is_error"):
                            err = o.get("result") or "failed"
                        take_usage(o)
    except OSError:
        pass
    return text, err, agent_sid, tok


def _call_gated(cmd, cwd, timeout, env=None, stdout=None):
    """subprocess.call in its OWN session, so a timeout kills the whole process group
    (bash -lc pipes, a wandered agent's children) — not just the leader that
    subprocess.run's timeout would SIGKILL, leaking the rest. Returns
    (exit_code, timed_out). stdout defaults to DEVNULL; pass an open file to capture
    (the agent turn streams its jsonl there). Never raises."""
    try:
        p = subprocess.Popen(cmd, cwd=cwd, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                             stdout=(stdout if stdout is not None else subprocess.DEVNULL),
                             stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, env=env, start_new_session=True)
    except OSError:
        return 1, False
    _live_register(p.pid)                        # so a delete can reap this own-session child
    try:
        p.communicate(timeout=timeout)
        return (p.returncode if p.returncode is not None else 1), False
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        try:
            os.killpg(os.getpgid(p.pid), signal.SIGKILL)
        except OSError:
            p.kill()
        try:
            p.communicate(timeout=5)
        except (subprocess.SubprocessError, OSError):
            pass
        return 1, True
    finally:
        _live_unregister(p.pid)


def _pipe_reader(fh, cap, tail, res, key, over, budget):
    """Drain one pipe of a command step INCREMENTALLY into a bounded buffer, so the
    runner's memory never depends on how much the child decides to write. Keeps at
    most `cap` bytes — the HEAD for stdout (that's what becomes the step's output),
    the TAIL for stderr (that's what the error line quotes) — and counts the rest so
    the truncation marker can be honest. Reading continues past the cap (a big but
    FINITE command must still finish and report its own exit code, exactly as before)
    until `budget`: crossing it means the child is a firehose, so `over` is set and
    the caller kills its process group. Records (kept_bytes, total_bytes) in res[key];
    never raises (it runs in a thread — an exception here would be swallowed)."""
    kept = bytearray()
    total = 0
    try:
        fd = fh.fileno()
        while True:
            try:
                chunk = os.read(fd, 65536)
            except (OSError, ValueError):
                break                                # pipe closed under us
            if not chunk:
                break                                # EOF: the child is done writing
            total += len(chunk)
            if tail:
                kept += chunk
                if len(kept) > cap:
                    del kept[:len(kept) - cap]
            elif len(kept) < cap:
                kept += chunk[:cap - len(kept)]
            if total > budget:
                over.set()
                break
    except Exception:                                # thread guard: never propagate
        pass
    res[key] = (bytes(kept), total)


def _join_all(threads, budget):
    """Join every thread within ONE shared budget (a per-thread timeout would let N
    stuck readers cost N × budget)."""
    end = time.time() + budget
    for t in threads:
        t.join(timeout=max(0.0, end - time.time()))


def _run_command_step(command, cwd, run_dir=None, stdin_data=None):
    """Run a command step: bash -lc in its own SESSION so a timeout can kill the
    WHOLE process group (pipes / backgrounded children), not just the bash leader
    that subprocess.run's timeout would SIGKILL. Captures BYTES (non-UTF-8 stdout —
    a stray binary in a git listing — must not raise UnicodeDecodeError and strand
    the step), incrementally and BOUNDED: communicate() used to hold the child's
    entire stdout in RAM and truncate afterwards, so `yes` (or any runaway loop) grew
    the runner to gigabytes for the whole CMD_TIMEOUT and then reported an empty
    output. Returns (text, exit_code, err). Never raises."""
    try:
        env = dict(os.environ)
        if run_dir:
            # The safe alternative to ref substitution: a command that needs an
            # upstream step's output reads it STRUCTURED out of the run's own
            # ledger.json (via this dir), instead of having agent-produced text
            # spliced into a shell line.
            env["PERVIO_WF_DIR"] = run_dir
        p = subprocess.Popen(["bash", "-lc", command], cwd=cwd, env=env,
                             stdin=(subprocess.PIPE if stdin_data is not None
                                    else subprocess.DEVNULL),
                             stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                             stderr=subprocess.PIPE, start_new_session=True)
    except OSError as e:
        return "", 1, str(e)[:300]
    _live_register(p.pid)                        # reapable by a delete mid-command
    if stdin_data is not None:
        # Feed from a thread: a payload bigger than the pipe buffer written from
        # THIS thread would deadlock against a command that is already flooding
        # stdout (both sides full, nobody draining). A command that exits without
        # reading (BrokenPipe) is its own business — the payload is best-effort.
        def _feed(fh=p.stdin, data=stdin_data.encode("utf-8")):
            try:
                fh.write(data)
                fh.close()
            except (BrokenPipeError, OSError, ValueError):
                pass
        threading.Thread(target=_feed, daemon=True).start()
    over = threading.Event()                     # set by whichever pipe floods first
    res = {}
    readers = [
        threading.Thread(target=_pipe_reader, daemon=True,
                         args=(p.stdout, CMD_MAX_OUTPUT, False, res, "out",
                               over, CMD_MAX_DRAIN)),
        threading.Thread(target=_pipe_reader, daemon=True,
                         args=(p.stderr, CMD_ERR_TAIL, True, res, "err",
                               over, CMD_MAX_DRAIN)),
    ]
    for t in readers:
        t.start()
    deadline = time.time() + CMD_TIMEOUT
    timed_out = flooded = False
    try:
        while True:
            if over.is_set():
                flooded = True
                break
            try:
                p.wait(timeout=0.05)
                break                            # exited on its own
            except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                pass
            if time.time() >= deadline:
                timed_out = True
                break
        if timed_out or flooded:
            # Kill the whole session group so nothing the command spawned lingers.
            try:
                os.killpg(os.getpgid(p.pid), signal.SIGKILL)
            except OSError:
                p.kill()
            try:
                p.wait(timeout=5)
            except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                pass
        _join_all(readers, 2)                    # ONE 2s grace for both, not 2s each
        if any(t.is_alive() for t in readers):
            # The command is gone but something still holds its stdout open: a
            # backgrounded grandchild that inherited the pipe. communicate() used to
            # sit here until CMD_TIMEOUT and then kill the group with an EMPTY result;
            # kill it now instead, and keep what the command actually wrote. (Only in
            # this stuck case — a well-behaved `daemon >/dev/null &` closed the pipe,
            # so its child is never touched.)
            try:
                os.killpg(p.pid, signal.SIGKILL)   # pgid == pid (own session)
            except OSError:
                pass
            _join_all(readers, 5)
        code = p.returncode if p.returncode is not None else 1
    except (OSError, ValueError) as e:
        return "", 1, str(e)[:300]
    finally:
        for fh in (p.stdout, p.stderr, p.stdin):
            if fh is None:
                continue
            try:
                fh.close()
            except OSError:
                pass
        _live_unregister(p.pid)
    if timed_out:
        return "", 1, "command timeout (%ds)" % int(CMD_TIMEOUT)
    out_b, out_total = res.get("out", (b"", 0))
    err_b, _ = res.get("err", (b"", 0))
    out = out_b.decode("utf-8", "replace")
    if out_total > len(out_b):
        out += "\n%s %d bytes]" % (CMD_TRUNC_MARK, out_total - len(out_b))
    if flooded:
        # Deliberately killed: never report this as a success with a quietly
        # shortened output — the step failed, and the reason is the flood.
        return out, 1, ("output limit exceeded (>%d bytes) — killed its process group"
                        % CMD_MAX_DRAIN)
    if code == 0:
        return out, 0, ""
    tail = err_b.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip()[-300:]
    return out, code, "exit %d: %s" % (code, tail or "command failed")


def _run_agent_turn(s, backend, step_dir, prompt, jsonl, spec, wf, sid, iso):
    """One step's agent turn via pervio-chat (the non-command path, verbatim from the
    inline original). Returns (ok, text, err, exit_code, agent_sid, tokens)."""
    default_dir = spec.get("dir", "~")
    b64 = base64.b64encode(prompt.encode("utf-8")).decode("ascii")
    cmd = [CHAT, "--text", "--backend", backend,
           "--dir", step_dir, "--prompt-b64", b64]
    allow = []                        # claude scoped-tool allowances (one --allow)
    if backend == "codex":
        cmd += ["--sandbox", s.get("sandbox", "workspace-write")]
    else:
        cmd += ["--perm", s.get("perm", "default")]
        if s.get("perm") == "plan":
            # Same door run_child already sealed for fan-out children
            # (2026-08-20 autopsy): a headless plan turn has nobody to answer
            # ExitPlanMode's "approve?", so the call errors and the turn dies
            # with an empty final message. TOP-LEVEL plan steps (merge/trends)
            # ran unprotected until 2026-08-22 — prompt-level begging was their
            # only guard. Every _run_agent_turn is orchestrator-spawned and
            # headless by construction; the App's interactive plan flow never
            # comes through here, so its approval sheet is untouched.
            cmd += ["--disallow", "ExitPlanMode"]
        # A step that needs the network (e.g. fetch an API) gets WebFetch
        # scoped-allowed — reaches the net without bypassPermissions. Claude only.
        if s.get("allowNetwork"):
            allow.append("WebFetch")
    # allowDispatch (P4): this step may spawn CHILD agent turns via pervio-dispatch,
    # which enforces depth/concurrency/budget/tier caps itself. Scope the parent so
    # it can call the dispatcher, put it on PATH, and hand it the run id + this step's
    # tier + the caps. Env stays None (inherit, byte-identical) for non-dispatch steps.
    disp_env = None
    if s.get("allowDispatch"):
        if backend != "codex":
            allow.append("Bash(pervio-dispatch *)")
        disp_top = (iso["top"] if iso else
                    _git(os.path.realpath(_expand_dir(s.get("dir", default_dir))),
                         "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel")[1])
        disp_env = dict(os.environ)
        disp_env["PATH"] = SELF_DIR + os.pathsep + os.environ.get("PATH", "")
        disp_env["PERVIO_WF_ID"] = wf
        disp_env["PERVIO_REPO_TOP"] = disp_top
        disp_env["PERVIO_STEP_ID"] = sid
        disp_env["PERVIO_PARENT_BACKEND"] = backend
        disp_env["PERVIO_PARENT_TIER"] = (s.get("sandbox", "workspace-write") if backend == "codex"
                                          else s.get("perm", "default"))
        # This step is a top-level agent (depth 0); don't inherit a stray
        # PERVIO_DISPATCH_DEPTH from the orchestrator's own env.
        disp_env["PERVIO_DISPATCH_DEPTH"] = "0"
        for envk, speck in (("PERVIO_BUDGET", "budgetUSD"), ("PERVIO_MAX_DEPTH", "maxDepth"),
                            ("PERVIO_MAX_CONCURRENCY", "maxConcurrency"),
                            ("PERVIO_MAX_DISPATCHES", "maxDispatches")):
            if spec.get(speck) is not None:
                disp_env[envk] = str(spec[speck])
    if allow:
        cmd += ["--allow", ",".join(allow)]
    model = resolve_model(s.get("model"))
    if model:
        if backend == "cursor":
            # Cursor's own names only (gpt-5.3-codex, composer-2.5…): a tier word
            # (flagship/balanced/…) resolves to a Claude id, which means nothing
            # to it — that step runs on Cursor's auto instead.
            raw = (s.get("model") or "").strip()
            if raw and resolve_model(raw) == raw:
                cmd += ["--model", raw]
        else:
            cmd += ["--model", model]
    if s.get("effort"):
        cmd += ["--effort", s["effort"]]

    with open(jsonl, "wb") as out_f:
        code, timed_out = _call_gated(cmd, None, AGENT_TIMEOUT, env=disp_env, stdout=out_f)
    if timed_out:
        # Killpg already reaped the whole group; report instead of hanging forever.
        # Still parse the partial jsonl for the session/thread id — the agent may
        # already have emitted it, and without it delete can't sweep this (real,
        # on-disk) timed-out conversation.
        _, _, agent_sid, tok = _step_output(jsonl, backend)
        return False, "", "agent turn timed out (>%ds)" % int(AGENT_TIMEOUT), code, agent_sid, tok
    text, err, agent_sid, tok = _step_output(jsonl, backend)
    return (code == 0 and not err), text, err, code, agent_sid, tok


def _publish_slug(spec):
    """Default publish dir name from the spec name: lowercased, runs of
    non-alphanumerics collapse to one '-'. Unicode-aware (str.isalnum), NOT
    ascii-only — otherwise every Chinese-named workflow ('论文晨报', '周报', …)
    would slug to '' and collide in one shared 'workflow' dir."""
    name = str(spec.get("name") or "").lower()
    slug = re.sub(r"-{2,}", "-",
                  "".join(c if c.isalnum() else "-" for c in name)).strip("-")
    return slug or "workflow"


def _publish_dir(spec, s):
    """Absolute target dir for a publishing step: its string value verbatim
    ('~'-expanded), or — publish: true — the per-workflow default under
    ~/.pervio/archives/. Validation already refused '..' traversal."""
    pub = s.get("publish")
    if isinstance(pub, str):
        return _expand_dir(pub)
    return os.path.join(HOME, ".pervio", "archives", _publish_slug(spec))


def _publish_output(spec, s, text):
    """Archive a published step's final output as an issue: <dir>/<date>.md,
    atomic like _write_atomic (per-writer tmp + os.replace), so a same-day
    rerun overwrites cleanly and a reader never sees a half-written issue.

    THE DATE COMES FROM THE ISSUE ITSELF — the first line's YYYY-MM-DD (the
    digest titles itself with the fetch's UTC-cycle date) — with the server's
    local date only as the fallback for reports that don't carry one. The
    filename used to be local-now unconditionally, which let a midnight-side
    run write 08-13's issue into 08-14.md: two clocks naming one thing.

    Empty (stripped) output writes NOTHING — a quiet day must not become a
    blank issue in the reader — and is not an error.
    Returns (absolute path or "", error or "")."""
    if not (text or "").strip():
        return "", ""
    d = _publish_dir(spec, s)
    first_line = text.strip().splitlines()[0]
    m = re.search(r"\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}", first_line)
    path = os.path.join(d, (m.group(0) if m else time.strftime("%Y-%m-%d")) + ".md")
    tmp = "%s.tmp.%d" % (path, os.getpid())
    try:
        os.makedirs(d, mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
        with open(tmp, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write(text)
        os.replace(tmp, path)
    except OSError as e:
        try:
            os.unlink(tmp)
        except OSError:
            pass
        return "", str(e)[:200]
    return os.path.realpath(path), ""


def _crash_run(rd, exc):
    """Last-resort net for the DETACHED runner. Its exceptions have no console: a
    crash used to kill the process silently, leaving ledger.status 'running' and the
    status file 'running' FOREVER — the phone spins on a run that is already dead, and
    nothing ever marks it failed. Land the run as failed, name the crash, and write
    the terminal status file. Best effort; never raises (the caller re-raises so the
    traceback still reaches runner.err)."""
    msg = "runner crashed: %s: %s" % (type(exc).__name__, str(exc)[:200])
    try:
        led = _read_json(os.path.join(rd, "ledger.json"))
        if led:
            for st in led.get("steps", []):
                if st.get("status") not in ("done", "failed", "skipped"):
                    st["status"] = "failed"
                    st["error"] = st.get("error") or msg
            led["status"] = "failed"
            led["error"] = msg
            _write_atomic(os.path.join(rd, "ledger.json"), led)
    except Exception:
        pass
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(rd, "status"), "w") as f:
            f.write("exited:1")
    except OSError:
        pass


def cmd_run(argv):
    global _LIVE_DIR
    o = _parse(argv, {"wf"})
    wf = o.get("wf", "")
    rd = _wf_dir(wf)
    _LIVE_DIR = os.path.join(rd, "live")   # where in-flight children register for reap
    try:
        _run_wf(wf, rd)
    except Exception as e:
        _crash_run(rd, e)
        raise                              # traceback -> runner.err, exit code stays 1


def _run_wf(wf, rd):
    spec = _read_json(os.path.join(rd, "spec.json"))
    ledger = _read_json(os.path.join(rd, "ledger.json"))
    steps = spec.get("steps", [])
    default_dir = spec.get("dir", "~")
    by_id = {s["id"]: (i, s) for i, s in enumerate(steps)}

    lock = threading.Lock()           # guards ledger + outputs/done/failed
    outputs = {}                      # stepId -> output text
    outputs_json = {}                 # stepId -> parsed structured value ({{id.json}})
    step_roots = {}                   # stepId -> its worktree root (isolated steps)
    done = set()                      # ids that finished OK
    failed = set()                    # ids that failed or were skipped
    notified = set()                  # push kinds already sent (see the outcome
                                      # ping at the end: a publish step's 「已送达」
                                      # IS the news, so the tail must not repeat it)

    def save():
        _write_atomic(os.path.join(rd, "ledger.json"), ledger)

    # ---- worktree isolation setup (None = SHARED, the exact legacy path) ----
    iso = _plan_isolation(spec, steps)
    git_lock = threading.Lock()       # serializes integration merges + wt remove/prune;
                                      # NEVER held together with `lock` (deadlock rule)
    top = iso["top"] if iso else ""
    integ_wt = os.path.join(rd, "wt", "integration")
    iso_branch = "pervio/wf-%s/integration" % wf
    if iso:
        rc, ref, _ = _git(top, "symbolic-ref", "-q", "HEAD")
        base_ref = ref or ""          # "" = detached HEAD -> merge-back disabled
        _git(top, "worktree", "prune")
        rc, _, e = _git(top, "worktree", "add", integ_wt, "-b", iso_branch, iso["base"])
        if rc != 0:
            _git(top, "worktree", "prune")
            rc, _, e = _git(top, "worktree", "add", integ_wt, "-b", iso_branch, iso["base"])
        if rc != 0:
            # Never silently degrade to shared (that's the clobber this exists to
            # stop) — fail the whole run with git's own words.
            for led in ledger["steps"]:
                led["status"] = "failed"
                led["error"] = "worktree isolation setup failed: %s" % (e or "git error")
            ledger["status"] = "failed"
            save()
            with open(os.path.join(rd, "status"), "w") as f:
                f.write("exited:1")
            return
        _, dirty, _ = _git(top, "status", "--porcelain")
        note = ("working tree had uncommitted changes; isolated steps see committed "
                "state only" if dirty else "")
        # Written at SETUP (not end) so a hard crash still leaves the branches
        # discoverable from the phone.
        ledger["git"] = {"branch": iso_branch, "baseRef": base_ref,
                         "baseCommit": iso["base"], "merged": False, "note": note}
        save()

    def run_step(idx, s):
        sid = s["id"]
        backend = s.get("backend", "claude")
        led = ledger["steps"][idx]
        # A fan-out step runs no agent turn of its own (its children get ephemeral
        # worktrees off HEAD), so it never takes a P3 step-worktree itself.
        iso_step = bool(iso and iso["in_repo"].get(sid) and not s.get("fanout"))
        reader = _is_reader(s) if iso_step else False
        stepbr = "pervio/wf-%s/steps/%s" % (wf, sid)
        with lock:
            snap = dict(outputs)      # deps are already done, so their outputs are set
            snap_json = dict(outputs_json)
            roots_snap = dict(step_roots)
            led["status"] = "running"
            # Wall-clock stamps per step: the phone shows durations, and the
            # efficiency suite can prove steps OVERLAPPED from the intervals
            # themselves instead of timing the whole run with a stopwatch
            # (a threshold that a loaded machine turns into a coin flip).
            led["startedAt"] = time.time()
            if iso:
                led["isolation"] = "worktree" if iso_step else "shared"
                if iso_step and not reader:
                    led["branch"] = stepbr
            save()

        # Working dir: isolated steps get a fresh worktree branched from the
        # integration tip (which already contains every finished dependency);
        # everything else keeps the raw spec dir exactly as before.
        fail_early = ""
        tip = ""
        wt = os.path.join(rd, "wt", "steps", sid)
        if iso_step:
            with git_lock:
                rc, tip, e = _git(integ_wt, "rev-parse", "HEAD")
            if rc != 0:
                fail_early = "isolation error: %s" % (e or "no integration tip")
            else:
                add = (["worktree", "add", "--detach", wt, tip] if reader
                       else ["worktree", "add", wt, "-b", stepbr, tip])
                rc, _, e = _git(top, *add)
                if rc != 0:           # stale registration from a crash: prune, retry once
                    _git(top, "worktree", "prune")
                    rc, _, e = _git(top, *add)
                if rc != 0:
                    fail_early = "could not create worktree: %s" % (e or "git error")
            if not fail_early and os.path.exists(os.path.join(wt, ".gitmodules")):
                # Timed: this is the one git op that fetches over the network.
                rc, _, e = _git(wt, "submodule", "update", "--init", "--recursive",
                                timeout=SETUP_TIMEOUT)
                if rc != 0:
                    fail_early = "submodule init failed: %s" % (e or "git error")
            pfx = iso["prefix"].get(sid, ".")
            step_dir = wt if pfx in (".", "") else os.path.join(wt, pfx)
            setup_dirt = set()
            if not fail_early and spec.get("setup"):
                src, setup_to = _call_gated(["bash", "-lc", spec["setup"]], step_dir,
                                            SETUP_TIMEOUT)
                if setup_to:
                    fail_early = "setup timed out (>%ds) — killed its process group" % int(SETUP_TIMEOUT)
                elif src != 0:
                    fail_early = ("setup failed (exit %d) — step ran in a fresh worktree "
                                  "checkout; untracked files (node_modules, .env, …) are "
                                  "absent there" % src)
                else:
                    # Snapshot what setup dropped (usually un-ignored provisioning
                    # like a .env copy): it's environment, not the step's work — it
                    # must neither be committed nor trip the reader rescue.
                    rc, dirt, _ = _git(wt, "status", "--porcelain")
                    if rc == 0:
                        setup_dirt = {l[3:].strip().strip('"') for l in dirt.splitlines() if len(l) > 3}
        else:
            step_dir = s.get("dir", default_dir)
        if fail_early:
            with lock:
                led["status"] = "failed"; led["error"] = fail_early
                failed.add(sid); save()
            return

        def _repoint(pid, t):
            proot = roots_snap.get(pid, "")
            # A producer's worktree is gone by now — re-point its absolute paths
            # at THIS step's own checkout (or the real repo for shared consumers).
            # Text refs only: rewriting substrings inside serialized JSON would
            # corrupt it, and structured values shouldn't carry worktree paths.
            mine = wt if iso_step else top
            if proot and proot.startswith(rd) and proot != mine and mine:
                t = t.replace(proot, mine)
            return t

        # {{item.output}}/{{item.json}} in a fan-out prompt name FIELDS of the
        # item, not a step called "item" — keep them (and the custom `as` var)
        # out of the ref pass so the per-item pass gets first touch.
        keep = set(RESERVED_IDS)
        if s.get("fanout"):
            keep.add((s.get("fanout") or {}).get("as", "item"))
        prompt = _resolve_refs(s.get("prompt", ""), snap, snap_json,
                               keep_ids=keep, repoint=_repoint)

        # Fan-out step: expand the upstream list into one child agent turn per item.
        # {{id.output}}/{{id.json}} refs are already resolved above; {{item}} was
        # kept intact and is substituted per-item inside _run_fanout.
        if s.get("fanout"):
            fok, fout, ferr, fjson = _run_fanout(s, prompt, snap, snap_json, led, lock,
                                                 save, wf, sid, backend, default_dir)
            with lock:
                led["output"] = fout
                led["bytes"] = len((fout or "").encode("utf-8"))
                if fok:
                    led["status"] = "done"; outputs[sid] = fout; done.add(sid)
                    if fjson is not None:
                        outputs_json[sid] = fjson
                else:
                    led["status"] = "failed"; led["error"] = ferr; failed.add(sid)
                save()
            if fok and fjson is not None:
                _write_json_artifact(rd, sid, fjson)
            return

        # Command step: run the shell line instead of an agent turn — stdout IS the
        # output. Deterministic, token-free (the data layer of scheduled digests).
        # Refs are NOT substituted into the command (upstream agent text into a
        # shell line is an injection vector); it's a fixed string, like `verify`.
        tok = {}
        parsed_val = None
        prior_sids = []
        if s.get("command"):
            jsonl = None
            agent_sid = ""
            retried_sid = ""
            # The stdin template is the command's DATA lane: refs resolve here
            # (both kinds), while the command line itself stays a fixed string.
            payload = None
            if s.get("stdin") is not None:
                payload = _resolve_refs(s["stdin"], snap, snap_json,
                                        keep_ids=RESERVED_IDS, repoint=_repoint)
            text, code, err = _run_command_step(s["command"], _expand_dir(step_dir), rd,
                                                stdin_data=payload)
            ok = (code == 0)
            # retry:N covers commands too — a curl'd fetch is deterministic in
            # shape but not in weather. No transient default here: without the
            # knob a command failure is the author's own exit code, kept as-is.
            want_retry = s.get("retry")
            extra = want_retry if (isinstance(want_retry, int)
                                   and not isinstance(want_retry, bool)) else 0
            att = 0
            while not ok and att < extra:
                att += 1
                with lock:
                    led["retried"] = True
                    save()
                text, code, err = _run_command_step(s["command"], _expand_dir(step_dir),
                                                    rd, stdin_data=payload)
                ok = (code == 0)
        else:
            jsonl = os.path.join(rd, "%s.jsonl" % sid)
            retried_sid = ""
            ok, text, err, code, agent_sid, tok = _run_agent_turn(s, backend, step_dir,
                                                                  prompt, jsonl, spec, wf,
                                                                  sid, iso)
            # Failure retries. Two regimes, one loop:
            #   * retry unset — the legacy contract, byte-stable: ONE re-run and
            #     only for transient weather (timeout / dropped connection /
            #     overloaded API), never for the agent being wrong.
            #   * retry: N — the author's own budget: up to N re-runs on ANY
            #     turn failure. Their step, their tokens.
            # Every replaced attempt's conversation is a real on-disk session
            # the CLI already created — ids are kept so delete's sweep reaches
            # them (the jsonl overwrite would otherwise orphan them).
            want_retry = s.get("retry")
            extra = want_retry if (isinstance(want_retry, int)
                                   and not isinstance(want_retry, bool)) else None
            attempt = 0
            while not ok:
                if extra is not None:
                    if attempt >= extra:
                        break
                elif attempt >= 1 or not TRANSIENT_RE.search(err or ""):
                    break
                attempt += 1
                if attempt == 1:
                    retried_sid = agent_sid
                elif agent_sid:
                    prior_sids.append(agent_sid)
                with lock:
                    led["retried"] = True
                    save()
                ok, text, err, code, agent_sid, tok = _run_agent_turn(s, backend, step_dir,
                                                                      prompt, jsonl, spec,
                                                                      wf, sid, iso)
            # outputSchema: the turn went FINE but the answer must also be the
            # declared shape. A wrong shape earns exactly one corrective re-run
            # (complaint + bad head appended); a second wrong answer fails the
            # step with the complaint. Runs before the commit/verify gates —
            # this is about the TEXT, and the re-run may still edit files.
            if ok and s.get("outputSchema") is not None:
                parsed_val, verr = _parse_and_validate(s["outputSchema"], text)
                if verr:
                    if agent_sid:
                        prior_sids.append(agent_sid)
                    try:
                        os.replace(jsonl, jsonl + ".badjson1")
                    except OSError:
                        pass
                    with lock:
                        led["schemaRetry"] = True
                        save()
                    fix = prompt + SCHEMA_FIX_NOTE % (verr, (text or "")[:1500])
                    ok, text, err, code, agent_sid, tok = _run_agent_turn(
                        s, backend, step_dir, fix, jsonl, spec, wf, sid, iso)
                    if ok:
                        parsed_val, verr = _parse_and_validate(s["outputSchema"], text)
                    if not ok or verr:
                        ok = False
                        parsed_val = None
                        err = err or ("输出不符合契约(schema): %s" % verr)

        # Isolated post-turn: commit the step's work (or rescue+fail a reader that
        # wrote), BEFORE the verify gate so failed-verify work survives on the branch.
        # Setup droppings are excluded throughout — they die with the worktree.
        head = tip
        if iso_step and ok:
            rc, dirty, _ = _git(wt, "status", "--porcelain")
            agent_paths = [l[3:].strip().strip('"') for l in dirty.splitlines()
                           if len(l) > 3 and l[3:].strip().strip('"') not in setup_dirt] \
                          if rc == 0 else []
            if reader:
                if agent_paths:
                    _git(wt, "add", "-A")
                    if setup_dirt:
                        _git(wt, "reset", "-q", "--", *sorted(setup_dirt))
                    _git(wt, "commit", "-m", "pervio wf %s step %s (rescued)" % (wf, sid))
                    _git(wt, "branch", stepbr)   # detached HEAD -> preserve the commit
                    ok = False
                    err = ("read-classified step modified files; work preserved on %s — "
                           "give it a write perm/sandbox" % stepbr)
            else:
                if agent_paths:
                    rc1, _, e1 = _git(wt, "add", "-A")
                    if setup_dirt:
                        _git(wt, "reset", "-q", "--", *sorted(setup_dirt))
                    rc2, _, e2 = _git(wt, "commit", "-m", "pervio wf %s step %s" % (wf, sid))
                    if rc1 or rc2:
                        ok = False
                        err = "commit failed: %s" % (e2 or e1 or "git error")
                if ok:                # the agent may also have committed on its own
                    rc, h, _ = _git(wt, "rev-parse", "HEAD")
                    if rc == 0 and h:
                        head = h

        # Verify gate: an optional shell command that must pass (exit 0) for the
        # step to count as done — e.g. "npm test" / "ruff check .". Runs in the
        # step's own dir (its worktree when isolated), AFTER the commit, so its
        # scratch droppings die with the worktree instead of polluting the merge.
        if ok and s.get("verify"):
            vrc, verify_to = _call_gated(["bash", "-lc", s["verify"]],
                                         _expand_dir(step_dir), VERIFY_TIMEOUT)
            if vrc != 0:
                ok = False
                err = ("verify timed out (>%ds) — killed its process group" % int(VERIFY_TIMEOUT)
                       if verify_to else "verify failed (exit %d)" % vrc)
                if iso_step and not reader and head != tip:
                    err += "; the step's work is committed on %s" % stepbr

        # Serialized merge into the integration branch. Merge the worktree's HEAD
        # OID (not the branch name — an agent can wander off its branch), then
        # re-point the step branch at what actually merged.
        if iso_step and not reader and ok and head and head != tip:
            with git_lock:
                rc, _, em = _git(integ_wt, "merge", "--no-ff",
                                 "-m", "pervio wf %s: step %s" % (wf, sid), head)
                if rc != 0:
                    _, files, _ = _git(integ_wt, "diff", "--name-only", "--diff-filter=U")
                    _git(integ_wt, "merge", "--abort")
                    ok = False
                    err = ("merge conflict — parallel steps changed: %s; work kept on %s"
                           % (", ".join(files.split()) if files else (em or "unknown"),
                              stepbr))

        # Publish: this step IS the workflow's publication — archive its final
        # output as today's issue the moment the STEP succeeds (`ok` is final
        # here: verify gate and merge have had their say; a LATER step failing
        # must not eat an already-good paper). A write failure (perms, disk)
        # goes to runner.err but never fails the step: the missing issue shows
        # on the home card, and the run's work survives.
        published = ""
        if ok and s.get("publish"):
            published, pub_err = _publish_output(spec, s, text)
            if pub_err:
                sys.stderr.write("pervio-orchestrate: step %s publish failed: %s\n"
                                 % (sid, pub_err))
            elif published:
                # The issue landed — ping the phone: "论文晨报 2026-08-12 已送达".
                # The date is the PUBLISHED FILE's name (which _publish_output
                # took from the issue's own title), not the wall clock: the push
                # must name the issue the tap will open. Recorded so the
                # workflow-level ping below stays quiet: this already told the
                # user the thing they care about.
                notified.add("publish")
                _push_notify(spec.get("name") or "Pervio",
                             _push_copy_published(
                                 os.path.splitext(os.path.basename(published))[0]),
                             thread="pervio-digest", device=ledger.get("device", ""))

        # Done is marked only AFTER the merge (git_lock released first — the two
        # locks are never nested), so dependents always branch from a tip that
        # already contains this step.
        with lock:
            led["output"] = text
            led["bytes"] = (os.path.getsize(jsonl) if jsonl and os.path.exists(jsonl)
                            else len((text or "").encode("utf-8")))
            if tok:
                led.update(tok)   # tokensIn/tokensOut — only when the backend said
            if agent_sid:
                # For delete's conversation sweep. realpath, because that's the cwd
                # the CLI records (its project-folder encoding resolves symlinks).
                led["sessionId"] = agent_sid
                led["sessionDir"] = os.path.realpath(_expand_dir(step_dir))
            if led.get("retried") and retried_sid and retried_sid != agent_sid:
                led["retriedSessionId"] = retried_sid   # attempt 1, swept on delete
            keep_sids = [p for p in prior_sids if p and p != agent_sid]
            if keep_sids:
                # A schema-retried step has an extra REAL conversation on disk
                # (attempt 1's) — recorded so delete's sweep reaches it too.
                led["priorSessionIds"] = sorted(set((led.get("priorSessionIds") or [])
                                                    + keep_sids))
            if ok:
                led["status"] = "done"; outputs[sid] = text; done.add(sid)
                if parsed_val is not None:
                    outputs_json[sid] = parsed_val
                if published:
                    led["publishedTo"] = published
                if iso_step:
                    step_roots[sid] = wt
            else:
                led["status"] = "failed"; led["error"] = err or ("exit %d" % code)
                failed.add(sid)
            led["endedAt"] = time.time()   # pairs with startedAt (see above)
            save()
        if ok and parsed_val is not None:
            _write_json_artifact(rd, sid, parsed_val)

        # Cleanup: successful worktrees go away (branch kept as audit); failed ones
        # stay under the run dir for inspection until the run is deleted.
        if iso_step and ok:
            with git_lock:
                _git(top, "worktree", "remove", "--force", wt)
                if not reader and head:
                    rc, cur, _ = _git(top, "rev-parse", "--verify", "refs/heads/" + stepbr)
                    if rc == 0 and cur != head:
                        _git(top, "branch", "-f", stepbr, head)

    # DAG scheduler: a step runs once all its deps are done; independent steps run
    # in parallel (capped). A step whose dependency failed is skipped. Ordering is
    # dependsOn ∪ {{id.output}} refs — ONE graph, _dep_edges, for every mode. Shared
    # runs used to gate on dependsOn alone, so a consumer could be submitted in the
    # same pass as its producer, substitute "" for the ref and still report `done`;
    # the ref IS the consumer's input, so it is always an edge.
    dep_all = iso["dep"] if iso else _dep_edges(steps)

    def eff_deps(s):
        return sorted(dep_all.get(s["id"], ()))

    # optional steps never block the graph: their failure (or skip) satisfies a
    # dependent, whose {{ref}}s then substitute empty — a DEGRADED downstream
    # beats a skipped one, and the three-step fanout charade that used to fake
    # this (prep + fanout-of-1 + tolerateAllFail) retires.
    optional_ids = {s["id"] for s in steps if s.get("optional")}
    # Resume (WF-2): steps seeded 'done+cached' by _start_wf ran in a PRIOR run —
    # their outputs are already in the ledger and json/, and they must not run
    # (or publish, or push) again.
    for i, s in enumerate(steps):
        st = ledger["steps"][i]
        if st.get("cached") and st.get("status") == "done":
            done.add(s["id"])
            outputs[s["id"]] = st.get("output", "")
            jp = os.path.join(rd, "json", "%s.json" % s["id"])
            if os.path.exists(jp):
                try:
                    with open(jp, "r") as jf:
                        outputs_json[s["id"]] = json.load(jf)
                except (OSError, ValueError):
                    pass

    pending = set(by_id) - done
    with concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=MAX_PARALLEL) as ex:
        running = {}
        while pending or running:
            moved = False
            for sid in list(pending):
                idx, s = by_id[sid]
                deps = eff_deps(s)
                with lock:
                    dep_failed = any(d in failed and d not in optional_ids
                                     for d in deps)
                    dep_done = all(d in done or (d in failed and d in optional_ids)
                                   for d in deps)
                if dep_failed:
                    with lock:
                        ledger["steps"][idx]["status"] = "skipped"
                        ledger["steps"][idx]["error"] = "a dependency failed"
                        failed.add(sid); save()
                    pending.discard(sid); moved = True
                elif dep_done:
                    pending.discard(sid)
                    running[ex.submit(run_step, idx, s)] = sid
                    moved = True
            if running:
                fin, _ = concurrent.futures.wait(
                    running, return_when=concurrent.futures.FIRST_COMPLETED)
                for f in fin:
                    sid = running.pop(f, None)
                    # run_step marks its own step done/failed; but an UNEXPECTED
                    # exception (it should catch its own) would otherwise be
                    # swallowed by the pool — leaving the step stuck 'running' and
                    # its dependents mislabeled "cycle". Surface it as a failure.
                    exc = f.exception()
                    if exc is not None and sid is not None:
                        idx = by_id[sid][0]
                        with lock:
                            st = ledger["steps"][idx]
                            if st["status"] not in ("done", "failed", "skipped"):
                                st["status"] = "failed"
                                st["error"] = "step crashed: %s" % str(exc)[:200]
                            failed.add(sid); save()
            elif pending and not moved:
                # nothing runnable and nothing changed: unsatisfiable deps / cycle
                with lock:
                    for sid in pending:
                        idx, _ = by_id[sid]
                        ledger["steps"][idx]["status"] = "failed"
                        ledger["steps"][idx]["error"] = "unsatisfiable dependency (cycle?)"
                        failed.add(sid)
                    save()
                pending.clear()

    # Merge back to where the user actually is — but only when every step landed
    # AND their checkout still sits on the same branch at the same commit as when
    # we started (never a detached HEAD). Any guard miss or nonzero merge leaves
    # the integration branch in place and says so; never retried, never forced.
    if iso:
        g = ledger["git"]
        keep_note = g.get("note", "")
        if failed:
            g["note"] = "not merged: a step failed (results on %s)" % iso_branch
        elif not g.get("baseRef"):
            g["note"] = "not merged: repo was on a detached HEAD (results on %s)" % iso_branch
        else:
            # The check and the merge are ONE step under the repository's
            # landing lock: without it another workflow can move the branch
            # between the two, or merge at the same instant (see _LandingLock).
            with _LandingLock(top) as landing:
                rc, ref_now, _ = _git(top, "symbolic-ref", "-q", "HEAD")
                rc2, head_now, _ = _git(top, "rev-parse", "HEAD")
                if not landing.held:
                    g["note"] = ("not merged: another workflow was still landing on this "
                                 "repository (results on %s)" % iso_branch)
                elif ref_now != g["baseRef"] or head_now != g["baseCommit"]:
                    g["note"] = ("not merged: the checkout moved during the run "
                                 "(results on %s)" % iso_branch)
                else:
                    # start_new_session so a concurrent `delete`'s killpg can never
                    # kill this mid-checkout and strand a half-rewritten user tree.
                    rc, _, em = _git(top, "merge", "--ff-only", iso_branch, new_session=True)
                    if rc == 0:
                        g["merged"] = True
                        g["note"] = "merged into %s" % g["baseRef"].replace("refs/heads/", "")
                    else:
                        g["note"] = "not merged: %s (results on %s)" % (
                            (em or "fast-forward refused").splitlines()[-1], iso_branch)
        if keep_note and not g["merged"]:
            g["note"] = (g["note"] + "; " + keep_note)
        with git_lock:
            # The integration worktree always goes; its branch (and the step
            # branches) survive UNLESS everything merged — then they're redundant
            # (all commits reachable from the user's branch) and get deleted.
            _git(top, "worktree", "remove", "--force", integ_wt)
            if g["merged"]:
                _, refs, _ = _git(top, "for-each-ref", "--format=%(refname:short)",
                                  "refs/heads/pervio/wf-%s/" % wf)
                for br in refs.splitlines():
                    if br.strip():
                        _git(top, "branch", "-D", br.strip())

    with lock:
        # An optional step's failure is a recorded degradation, not a failed
        # run: the whole point of the flag is that the workflow stands without it.
        hard_failed = sorted(f for f in failed if f not in optional_ids)
        ledger["status"] = "failed" if hard_failed else "done"
        save()
    with open(os.path.join(rd, "status"), "w") as f:
        f.write("exited:%d" % (1 if hard_failed else 0))

    # Workflow-level outcome ping — EXACTLY ONE per run, both outcomes.
    #
    # It used to fire only on failure, on the grounds that success was covered by
    # "the app's own completion banner". That is no longer true and was never
    # good: the app can only banner while iOS has not suspended it, and when it
    # DID banner the user got two notifications for one workflow (the app's
    # "Task finished" next to this script's push). The app now defers to this
    # push (CompletionNotifier.serverPushArmed), so silence here is silence
    # everywhere — which is why success must ping too.
    #
    # The one exception is a workflow that already published: 「今天的晨报已送达」
    # is the same news told better, so `notified` suppresses a second buzz.
    # Either way the title is the workflow's own name — that is what tells the
    # user WHICH of their scheduled jobs is talking.
    name = spec.get("name") or "Pervio"
    if hard_failed:
        _push_notify(name, _push_copy_failed(hard_failed), thread="pervio-wf-" + wf,
                     device=ledger.get("device", ""))
    elif "publish" not in notified:
        _push_notify(name, _push_copy_done(len(steps)), thread="pervio-wf-" + wf,
                     device=ledger.get("device", ""))


# ---------- status / tail / list ----------

def _reconcile_dead(rd, led):
    """The ledger says running but the runner is DEAD (kill -9, reboot, power
    loss): flip the record to the truth, once, atomically. 2026-08-22 audit P1:
    list/status/tail all echoed the stale 'running' forever, iOS spun on it, and
    the pruner skipped the corpse for eternity. Only ever called AFTER _wf_alive
    said no — the runner being dead is exactly what makes this write race-free."""
    for st in led.get("steps") or []:
        if st.get("status") == "running":
            st["status"] = "failed"
            st["error"] = st.get("error") or "runner died (kill or reboot) mid-step"
        elif st.get("status") == "pending":
            st["status"] = "skipped"
            st["error"] = st.get("error") or "runner died before this step started"
    led["status"] = "failed"
    _write_atomic(os.path.join(rd, "ledger.json"), led)
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(rd, "status"), "w") as f:
            f.write("exited:1")
    except OSError:
        pass
    return led


def cmd_status(argv):
    o = _parse(argv, {"wf"})
    rd = _wf_dir(o.get("wf", ""))
    if not os.path.isdir(rd):
        _die("no such workflow", 3)
    led = _read_json(os.path.join(rd, "ledger.json"))
    if led.get("status") == "running" and not _wf_alive(o.get("wf", "")):
        led = _reconcile_dead(rd, led)
    sys.stdout.write(json.dumps(led) + "\n")


def cmd_spec(argv):
    """The spec a workflow was started from, verbatim — lets the phone re-run a
    past workflow by seeding its editor with the original steps (incl. fanout /
    dispatch knobs) instead of rebuilding from the market every time."""
    o = _parse(argv, {"wf"})
    rd = _wf_dir(o.get("wf", ""))
    if not os.path.isdir(rd):
        _die("no such workflow", 3)
    sys.stdout.write(json.dumps(_read_json(os.path.join(rd, "spec.json"))) + "\n")


def cmd_tail(argv):
    o = _parse(argv, {"wf"})
    rd = _wf_dir(o.get("wf", ""))
    led_path = os.path.join(rd, "ledger.json")
    for _ in range(40):
        if os.path.exists(led_path):
            break
        time.sleep(0.05)
    if not os.path.exists(led_path):
        _die("no such workflow", 3)
    last = None
    last_out = time.time()
    while True:
        led = _read_json(led_path)
        snap = json.dumps(led, ensure_ascii=False)
        if snap != last:
            sys.stdout.write(snap + "\n")
            sys.stdout.flush()
            last = snap
            last_out = time.time()
        elif time.time() - last_out >= 10:
            # Ten silent seconds is also when a DEAD runner shows itself: a live
            # one writes the ledger every step, a corpse never will. Checking
            # here (not every 0.5s pass) keeps the ps probe off the hot loop.
            if led.get("status") == "running" and not _wf_alive(o.get("wf", "")):
                led = _reconcile_dead(rd, led)
                sys.stdout.write(json.dumps(led, ensure_ascii=False) + "\n")
                sys.stdout.flush()
                break
            # A step can sit silent for minutes (e.g. codex "thinking"), during
            # which the ledger doesn't change and we'd emit nothing — long enough
            # for the phone to trip its idle timeout and reconnect. Emit a
            # heartbeat at the line boundary to keep the channel warm; the app
            # decodes it as a blank ledger and drops it (wfId guard).
            sys.stdout.write('{"type":"heartbeat"}\n')
            sys.stdout.flush()
            last_out = time.time()
        if led.get("status") in ("done", "failed"):
            break
        time.sleep(0.5)


TPL = os.path.join(HOME, ".pervio", "templates")


def _tpl_suggested_time(v):
    """The wrapper's daily time as canonical "HH:MM", or None to omit the field.

    The phone's market calls a template a 刊物 — subscribe once, read the home
    card — when its spec publishes AND it ships with a daily time. Server
    templates had no way to say the time, so a publishing template landed on the
    capability shelf with no subscribe button at all.

    Zero-padded on the way out because the app parses with a strict "HH:mm", so a
    hand-written "6:30" has to arrive as "06:30" or it is silently ignored.
    Anything that is not a 24h time — a typo, a number, a list, a string with a
    NUL in it — is OMITTED rather than passed through or turned into an error:
    the app must be able to tell "no suggestion" from "a suggestion I can't
    read", and a template with a bad time is still a perfectly runnable template.
    """
    if not isinstance(v, str):
        return None
    try:
        hh, mm = _parse_hhmm(v)
    except ValueError:
        return None
    return "%02d:%02d" % (hh, mm)


def cmd_models(argv):
    """The model paper: tier table + chat quick-pick menu, straight from
    _pervio_lib. The phone reads it once per connect and caches it."""
    print(json.dumps(models_menu(), ensure_ascii=False))


def cmd_templates(argv):
    """The user's personal workflow market, server-side: any *.json under
    ~/.pervio/templates (written by hand, by the editor's Save-as-template, or
    by Claude in a chat) shows up in the app with zero app changes. A file is
    either a bare spec or a market-item wrapper {name, summary, spec}; the
    WRAPPER may additionally carry "expose": true + "slug" + "params" (the F1
    declaration pervio-mcp turns into a named pervio_run_<slug> tool — wrapper
    fields, so a bare-spec template cannot declare them) and "suggestedTime"
    (the daily time the app's market needs to offer a subscribe button; wrapper
    field for the same reason). This listing passes {name, summary, spec}
    through unchanged, plus suggestedTime canonicalised to "HH:MM" and OMITTED
    when absent or unreadable. Broken files are LISTED with their error instead
    of hidden — a template Claude just wrote must be debuggable from the phone.

    Third kind — playbooks: {"kind":"playbook","name","summary","body"} is
    methodology markdown LOADED INTO A CONVERSATION, never started. Listed as
    {file,name,summary,kind,bodyChars} plus the body verbatim (playbooks are
    small; one trip beats a follow-up fetch), with no spec validation. A
    missing/empty body or an unknown kind is listed with its error like any
    broken file. Files WITHOUT a kind field keep the exact legacy shape
    (kind defaults to workflow; no new fields grafted on)."""
    if argv[:1] == ["remove"]:
        # `templates remove --file <name>.json`: delete ONE of the person's own
        # template files (the market's "Mine" shelf, 2026-10-20). The name is a
        # bare filename inside ~/.pervio/templates — no path, no "..": a template
        # is the only thing this can ever remove. A file that isn't there is
        # reported, not invented.
        o = _parse(argv[1:], {"file"})
        fn = o.get("file", "")
        if not fn or "/" in fn or "\\" in fn or fn in (".", "..") or not fn.endswith(".json") or fn.startswith("."):
            _die("templates remove needs --file <name>.json (a bare filename)", 2)
        p = os.path.join(TPL, fn)
        if not os.path.isfile(p):
            sys.stdout.write(json.dumps({"removed": False, "file": fn, "reason": "no such template"}) + "\n")
            sys.exit(3)
        os.remove(p)
        sys.stdout.write(json.dumps({"removed": True, "file": fn}) + "\n")
        return
    if not argv or argv[0] != "list":
        _die("usage: pervio-orchestrate templates list | templates remove --file <name>.json")
    items = []
    if os.path.isdir(TPL):
        for fn in sorted(os.listdir(TPL)):
            if not fn.endswith(".json"):
                continue
            p = os.path.join(TPL, fn)
            try:
                mtime = int(os.path.getmtime(p))
            except OSError:
                continue
            entry = {"file": fn, "mtime": mtime}
            raw = _read_json(p)
            kind = raw.get("kind") if isinstance(raw, dict) else None
            if kind == "playbook":
                body = raw.get("body")
                if "spec" in raw:
                    # Nothing legitimate puts a spec in a playbook — surface the
                    # smuggle loudly instead of silently stripping it (the app
                    # then shows a broken row, and archives/start never see it).
                    entry.update({"ok": False, "name": str(raw.get("name") or fn),
                                  "error": "playbook must not carry a spec"})
                    items.append(entry)
                    continue
                if isinstance(body, str) and body.strip():
                    entry.update({"ok": True, "name": str(raw.get("name") or fn),
                                  "summary": str(raw.get("summary") or "")[:500],
                                  "kind": "playbook", "bodyChars": len(body),
                                  "body": body})
                else:
                    entry.update({"ok": False, "name": fn,
                                  "error": "playbook needs a non-empty string body"})
                items.append(entry)
                continue
            if kind not in (None, "workflow"):
                entry.update({"ok": False, "name": fn,
                              "error": "unknown template kind %r" % (kind,)})
                items.append(entry)
                continue
            summary = ""
            spec = raw
            sug = None
            if isinstance(raw, dict) and isinstance(raw.get("spec"), dict):
                summary = str(raw.get("summary") or "")[:500]
                # Read off the WRAPPER only — never spec["suggestedTime"] — so an
                # imported/agent-written spec body cannot declare its own daily
                # time, exactly as it cannot declare expose/slug/params.
                sug = _tpl_suggested_time(raw.get("suggestedTime"))
                spec = raw["spec"]
            try:
                if not raw:
                    raise ValueError("not valid JSON")
                _validate_spec(spec)
                entry.update({"ok": True, "name": spec.get("name") or fn,
                              "steps": len(spec.get("steps") or []),
                              "summary": summary, "spec": spec})
                if sug:
                    entry["suggestedTime"] = sug
            except Exception as e:
                entry.update({"ok": False, "name": fn, "error": str(e)[:200]})
            items.append(entry)
    items.sort(key=lambda i: -i.get("mtime", 0))
    sys.stdout.write(json.dumps(items, ensure_ascii=False) + "\n")


def _spec_pub_dirs(spec):
    """Every dir a spec publishes into, verbatim spellings: the legacy top-level
    display.archiveDir first (so when a publish step names the same dir the
    entry keeps the spelling the user originally declared), then each publish
    step's dir (its string value, or the default ~/.pervio/archives/<slug> for
    publish:true). A '..'-traversal dir is dropped outright: an IMPORTED spec
    must not point the reader outside where the user thinks it publishes."""
    if not isinstance(spec, dict):
        return []
    disp = spec.get("display") if isinstance(spec.get("display"), dict) else {}
    dirs = []
    d = disp.get("archiveDir")
    if isinstance(d, str) and d.strip() and _dir_ok(d):
        dirs.append(d)
    steps = spec.get("steps")
    for s in (steps if isinstance(steps, list) else []):
        if not isinstance(s, dict):
            continue
        pub = s.get("publish")
        if pub is True:
            dirs.append("~/.pervio/archives/" + _publish_slug(spec))
        elif isinstance(pub, str) and pub.strip() and _dir_ok(pub):
            dirs.append(pub)
    return dirs


def cmd_archives(argv):
    """The user's publications, two sources per spec: a step marked `publish`
    (dir = its string value, or the default ~/.pervio/archives/<slug> the
    orchestrator writes into) and the legacy top-level display.archiveDir
    (compat layer — the paper digest's spec still declares it, zero-change
    migration). Sources: schedule specs first, then templates (inner spec for
    wrapped files) — a schedule IS a template put to work, so when both point
    at the same dir (dedupe by realpath) the schedule's entry wins; archiveDir
    and a publish step naming the same dir are ONE publication, not two. A
    publication that exactly ONE schedule publishes into also carries that
    schedule's vitals (scheduleId/scheduleEnabled/lastStatus/lastError/
    lastFiredAt/nextDue) so the home card can say "this morning's issue failed"
    without a second round trip; zero or several matching schedules leave the
    fields absent (an ambiguous join would pin the wrong schedule's failure on
    an innocent publication)."""
    if not argv or argv[0] != "list":
        _die("usage: pervio-orchestrate archives list")
    pubs = {}                     # realpath(dir) -> entry; first writer (a schedule) wins

    def consider(spec):
        if not isinstance(spec, dict):
            return
        disp = spec.get("display") if isinstance(spec.get("display"), dict) else {}
        for d in _spec_pub_dirs(spec):
            key = os.path.realpath(_expand_dir(d))
            if key in pubs:
                continue
            files = []
            try:
                names = os.listdir(key)
            except OSError:
                names = []        # dir not created yet — a publication with no issues
            for fn in names:
                if not fn.endswith(".md"):
                    continue
                try:
                    files.append({"file": fn,
                                  "mtime": int(os.path.getmtime(os.path.join(key, fn)))})
                except OSError:
                    continue
            files.sort(key=lambda f: -f["mtime"])
            pubs[key] = {"name": spec.get("name") or "", "dir": d,
                         "display": disp, "actions": spec.get("actions") or [],
                         "files": files[:90]}   # ~a quarter of a daily — plenty for the phone

    sched_by_key = {}             # realpath(dir) -> [schedule ids publishing there]
    if os.path.isdir(SCHED):
        for sid in sorted(os.listdir(SCHED)):
            if not VALID_ID.match(sid):
                continue
            spec = _read_json(os.path.join(SCHED, sid, "spec.json"))
            consider(spec)
            for d in _spec_pub_dirs(spec):
                ids = sched_by_key.setdefault(os.path.realpath(_expand_dir(d)), [])
                if sid not in ids:   # archiveDir + publish on one dir is ONE schedule
                    ids.append(sid)
    if os.path.isdir(TPL):
        for fn in sorted(os.listdir(TPL)):
            if not fn.endswith(".json"):
                continue
            raw = _read_json(os.path.join(TPL, fn))
            # A playbook is conversation methodology — a spec smuggled inside
            # its wrapper must not mint a phantom publication whose reader
            # would surface an arbitrary dir on the phone (cmd_templates
            # rejects such wrappers outright; this is the same gate here).
            if isinstance(raw, dict) and raw.get("kind") == "playbook":
                continue
            spec = (raw["spec"] if isinstance(raw, dict) and isinstance(raw.get("spec"), dict)
                    else raw)
            consider(spec)
    # The schedule join: exactly one schedule publishing into the dir pins its
    # vitals on the entry; zero or several leave the fields absent.
    now = _sched_now()
    for key, entry in pubs.items():
        ids = sched_by_key.get(key) or []
        if len(ids) != 1:
            continue
        sch = _read_sched(ids[0])
        if not sch:
            continue
        try:
            nxt = _occurrence(sch, now, +1)
        except ValueError:
            nxt = None
        entry.update({"scheduleId": sch.get("id") or ids[0],
                      "scheduleEnabled": bool(sch.get("enabled")),
                      "lastStatus": _last_status(sch),
                      "lastError": sch.get("lastError") or "",
                      "lastFiredAt": int(sch.get("lastFiredAt") or 0),
                      "nextDue": int(nxt.timestamp()) if nxt else 0})
    # A publication earns its home card by being USED: at least one issue on
    # disk, or a live (enabled) subscription publishing into it. A template that
    # merely ships with a publish step — the seeds, or a spec Claude wrote and
    # nobody ran — stays a market item: a fresh user used to open the app to
    # three empty cards (2026-10-11). A paused subscription with no issues drops
    # off the home until it is resumed; an issue, once printed, keeps the card.
    def _in_use(key, entry):
        if entry["files"]:
            return True
        for sid in sched_by_key.get(key) or []:
            sch = _read_sched(sid)
            if sch and sch.get("enabled"):
                return True
        return False
    items = [e for k, e in pubs.items() if _in_use(k, e)]
    # Freshest publication first; one that hasn't produced an issue yet sorts last.
    items.sort(key=lambda i: (0, -i["files"][0]["mtime"]) if i["files"] else (1, 0))
    sys.stdout.write(json.dumps(items, ensure_ascii=False) + "\n")


def cmd_list(argv):
    items = []
    if os.path.isdir(WF):
        for wid in os.listdir(WF):
            led = _read_json(os.path.join(WF, wid, "ledger.json"))
            if led:
                if led.get("status") == "running" and not _wf_alive(wid):
                    led = _reconcile_dead(os.path.join(WF, wid), led)
                items.append({k: led.get(k)
                              for k in ("wfId", "name", "status", "startedAt", "scheduleId")})
    items.sort(key=lambda i: i.get("startedAt", 0), reverse=True)
    sys.stdout.write(json.dumps(items) + "\n")


def cmd_delete(argv):
    o = _parse(argv, {"wf"})
    wf = o.get("wf", "")
    rd = _wf_dir(wf)                # validates the id (no path traversal)
    if not os.path.isdir(rd):
        _die("no such workflow", 3)
    residue = _delete_run(wf, rd)
    if residue:
        # rmtree(ignore_errors=True) used to swallow a denied unlink and this line
        # reported success anyway: the run vanished from the phone's list while its
        # ledger and transcripts stayed on disk forever. Say what survived instead.
        sys.stderr.write("pervio-orchestrate: delete of %s did NOT finish — still on disk:\n%s\n"
                         % (wf, "\n".join("  " + r for r in residue)))
        sys.stdout.write(json.dumps({"deleted": False, "wfId": wf, "reason": "residue",
                                     "remaining": residue}) + "\n")
        sys.exit(1)
    sys.stdout.write(json.dumps({"deleted": wf}) + "\n")


def _proc_start(pid):
    """A stable per-PROCESS token (its start time) that DIFFERS if the pid is reused
    by a later process — the missing piece that lets us tell 'our still-running
    session leader' from 'an unrelated login that inherited the recycled pgid after
    a reboot'. Linux: /proc/<pid>/stat field 22. macOS/BSD: `ps -o lstart=`.
    Returns "" when it can't be determined (old run, unknown platform)."""
    try:
        with open("/proc/%d/stat" % pid) as f:
            data = f.read()
        # comm (field 2) is parenthesized and may itself contain ')'/spaces; split
        # after the LAST ')' so the remaining fields align. starttime is field 22,
        # i.e. index 19 counting from field 3 (state) at index 0.
        return data[data.rfind(")") + 1:].split()[19]
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
        pass
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(["ps", "-o", "lstart=", "-p", str(pid)],
                           stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, capture_output=True, text=True,
                           timeout=5)
        return (p.stdout or "").strip()
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return ""


def _pgid_identity_ok(rd, pgid):
    """True only if `pgid` is alive AND is really THIS run's process group (its start
    token matches what we recorded), so a signal is safe. None when we can't prove
    identity (no recorded token — an old run or a platform without start times);
    callers fall back to the age cap. False when the pid is dead or provably reused."""
    try:
        os.killpg(pgid, 0)
    except OSError:
        return False                           # dead group
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(rd, "pgid.start")) as f:
            saved = f.read().strip()
    except OSError:
        saved = ""
    if not saved:
        return None                            # can't prove identity
    return _proc_start(pgid) == saved          # match => ours; mismatch => reused


def _live_register(pid):
    """Record an in-flight child's process-group leader (pid == pgid, it's a session
    leader) in _LIVE_DIR, tagged with its start token, so _delete_run can reap it —
    these children run in their own session and so escape the runner's pgid. No-op
    until cmd_run sets _LIVE_DIR."""
    if not _LIVE_DIR:
        return
    try:
        os.makedirs(_LIVE_DIR, mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
        with open(os.path.join(_LIVE_DIR, str(pid)), "w") as f:
            f.write(_proc_start(pid))
    except OSError:
        pass


def _live_unregister(pid):
    """Drop a child's registry entry once it has exited (normal path)."""
    if not _LIVE_DIR:
        return
    try:
        os.remove(os.path.join(_LIVE_DIR, str(pid)))
    except OSError:
        pass


def _reap_live_children(rd):
    """Reap any still-alive child groups a delete would otherwise orphan (they run in
    their own session, outside the runner's pgid). Uses the same reused-pid safety as
    the runner: only signal a pid whose start token still matches what we recorded, so
    a recycled pid belonging to an unrelated process is never touched."""
    live = os.path.join(rd, "live")
    try:
        names = os.listdir(live)
    except OSError:
        return
    for name in names:
        try:
            pid = int(name)
        except ValueError:
            continue
        try:
            with open(os.path.join(live, name)) as f:
                tok = f.read().strip()
        except OSError:
            continue
        if not tok or _proc_start(pid) != tok:   # gone, or the pid was reused — skip
            continue
        try:
            os.killpg(pid, signal.SIGTERM)
            for _ in range(20):                  # up to 2s for it to exit
                time.sleep(0.1)
                try:
                    os.killpg(pid, 0)
                except ProcessLookupError:
                    break
            else:
                os.killpg(pid, signal.SIGKILL)
        except OSError:
            pass


def _residue(path, cap=20):
    """Everything still on disk under `path` after a teardown attempt, sorted and
    capped. An empty list is the ONLY proof that a delete actually finished —
    shutil.rmtree(ignore_errors=True) returns None whether it removed the tree or
    silently gave up on a denied unlink."""
    if not os.path.exists(path):
        return []
    left = [path]
    for root, dirs, files in os.walk(path):
        for n in sorted(dirs) + sorted(files):
            left.append(os.path.join(root, n))
            if len(left) >= cap:
                left.append("... (more)")
                return left
    return left


def _delete_run(wf, rd):
    """Full teardown of one run (kill, rmtree, git sweep, conversation sweep) —
    shared by cmd_delete and the schedule pruner. Returns the list of paths that
    are STILL on disk afterwards; empty means the run really is gone."""
    # Read the ledger FIRST — rmtree destroys the only pointer to the project
    # repo whose worktrees/branches this run may have created.
    led = _read_json(os.path.join(rd, "ledger.json"))
    # If it's still running, stop the detached runner group and WAIT for it to
    # die before deleting: a live child mid-`worktree add` would otherwise write
    # into the dir while rmtree walks it. Escalate to SIGKILL after 5s.
    status = ""
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(rd, "status")) as f:
            status = f.read().strip()
    except OSError:
        pass
    if status == "running":
        try:
            with open(os.path.join(rd, "pgid")) as f:
                pid = int(f.read().strip())
            # Only signal a group we can vouch for. A crash/reboot can leave a stale
            # 'running' whose pgid the OS has since handed to an unrelated process;
            # blindly SIGTERM→SIGKILL there would murder a bystander. Signal when the
            # start token proves it's ours, or (no token) when the run is recent
            # enough to still plausibly be ours; otherwise just reclaim the dir.
            ident = _pgid_identity_ok(rd, pid)
            may_signal = ident is True
            if ident is None:               # unprovable — gate on age, like _wf_alive
                started = led.get("startedAt", 0)
                may_signal = not (started and (time.time() - started) > WF_MAX_AGE)
            if may_signal:
                os.killpg(pid, signal.SIGTERM)
                for _ in range(50):             # up to 5s for the group to exit
                    time.sleep(0.1)
                    try:
                        os.killpg(pid, 0)       # raises when the group is gone
                    except ProcessLookupError:
                        break
                else:
                    try:
                        os.killpg(pid, signal.SIGKILL)
                    except OSError:
                        pass
                    time.sleep(0.2)
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            pass
    # Killing the runner's pgid above does NOT reach children that run in their own
    # session (agent turns, command/setup/verify shells, fan-out children) — reap those
    # too before rmtree, so a delete mid-run actually stops the work instead of leaving
    # orphans writing into the dir we're about to remove. Unconditional: a crashed
    # runner leaves registry entries behind even when status is no longer "running".
    _reap_live_children(rd)
    # Codex fan-out/teststep children's thread ids live ONLY in their streams
    # inside this directory — take the register before the building comes down
    # (the sweep runs after the rmtree, by design: conversations must not block
    # the delete, and the claude half never needed the dir).
    codex_threads = _codex_child_threads(rd)
    shutil.rmtree(rd, ignore_errors=True)
    residue = _residue(rd)      # measured BEFORE the sweeps, which never recreate rd
    # Deleting the run is the explicit "discard its results" action: clear the
    # stale worktree registrations (their dirs just went away with rd) and every
    # pervio/wf-<id>/* branch. Run UNCONDITIONALLY whenever the dir is a git repo —
    # P3 isolation (led.git), P4 dispatch, and fan-out all leave worktrees/branches
    # under that same namespace, and dispatch/fan-out happen even when led.git is absent.
    rc, top, _ = _git(os.path.realpath(_expand_dir(led.get("dir", "~"))),
                      "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel")
    if rc == 0 and top:
        _git(top, "worktree", "prune")
        _, refs, _ = _git(top, "for-each-ref", "--format=%(refname:short)",
                          "refs/heads/pervio/wf-%s/" % wf)
        for br in refs.splitlines():
            if br.strip():
                _git(top, "branch", "-D", br.strip())
    _sweep_conversations(wf, led, codex_threads)
    return residue


CODEX_SESS = os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), ".codex", "sessions")


def _sweep_codex_sessions(thread_ids):
    """Delete codex rollout files for the given thread ids. Codex's store
    (~/.codex/sessions/<Y>/<M>/<D>/rollout-*-<id>.jsonl, id in the filename)
    sat OUTSIDE every sweep since P4: claude children were reaped precisely and
    wholesale, codex children lived forever — 21 month-old audit ghosts were
    still haunting the project list on 2026-08-24. Best-effort like the claude
    half: a miss never blocks the delete."""
    import glob as _glob
    for tid in thread_ids:
        if not tid or not re.match(r"^[A-Za-z0-9-]{8,64}$", tid):
            continue
        for f in _glob.glob(os.path.join(CODEX_SESS, "**", "*%s*.jsonl" % tid),
                            recursive=True):
            try:
                os.remove(f)
            except OSError:
                pass


def _codex_child_threads(rd):
    """Thread ids of every CODEX fan-out/teststep child in this run — parsed
    from their on-disk streams (thread.started), because children never write
    the ledger. The new audit template mints eight codex auditors per run;
    without this, every deleted audit leaves eight ghosts."""
    import glob as _glob
    out = set()
    for path in (_glob.glob(os.path.join(rd, "dispatch", "**", "out.jsonl*"), recursive=True)
                 + _glob.glob(os.path.join(rd, "teststep", "*", "out.jsonl*"))):
        try:
            with open(path, errors="replace") as f:
                for line in f:
                    if '"thread.started"' not in line:
                        continue
                    try:
                        o = json.loads(line)
                    except ValueError:
                        continue
                    if o.get("type") == "thread.started" and o.get("thread_id"):
                        out.add(str(o["thread_id"]))
                    break
        except OSError:
            continue
    return out


def _sweep_conversations(wf, led, codex_threads=()):
    """Deleting a workflow also discards the CONVERSATIONS its agents created, so
    they don't pile up in the phone's history drawer. Two mechanisms:
      (a) precise — each step's recorded sessionId/sessionDir, removed via
          pervio-sessions delete (reusing its encoding + traversal guards); this is
          what catches shared-mode steps that ran in the user's real project dir.
      (b) wholesale — every ~/.claude/projects folder whose encoded name is under
          THIS workflow's own dir (step worktrees, fan-out/dispatch scratch dirs);
          those cwds exist only for this run, so the whole folders go, ids unneeded.
    Codex sessions are recorded but left in place (pervio-sessions delete handles
    claude session files only). Best-effort throughout — a sweep failure must
    never block the delete itself."""
    for st in (led.get("steps") or []):
        s_dir = st.get("sessionDir")
        # A retried step has EXTRA real conversations on disk: transient
        # attempt 1 (retriedSessionId), any schema-retry priors
        # (priorSessionIds) — their jsonls were overwritten/archived but the
        # CLI session store is separate. Sweep them all.
        sids = ([st.get("sessionId"), st.get("retriedSessionId")]
                + list(st.get("priorSessionIds") or []))
        if st.get("backend") == "codex":
            # The codex store is file-per-thread — delete directly (pervio-
            # sessions delete only speaks claude's store).
            _sweep_codex_sessions([x for x in sids if x])
            continue
        for s_id in sids:
            if not s_id or not s_dir:
                continue
            try:
                r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.join(SELF_DIR, "pervio-sessions"),
                                    "delete", "--dir", s_dir, "--session", s_id],
                                   stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, capture_output=True, timeout=15)
                # Best-effort (a failure must never block the delete), but no longer
                # SILENT: a leftover conversation now leaves a trace to clean up later.
                if r.returncode != 0:
                    sys.stderr.write("pervio-orchestrate: session sweep failed for %s in %s "
                                     "(rc=%d): %s\n" % (s_id, s_dir, r.returncode,
                                     (r.stderr or b"").decode("utf-8", "replace").strip()[:200]))
            except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as e:
                sys.stderr.write("pervio-orchestrate: session sweep error for %s: %s\n"
                                 % (s_id, str(e)[:200]))
    # Codex fan-out/teststep children (the audit template's jurors): thread
    # ids were collected by _delete_run BEFORE the rmtree — their streams are
    # already gone by the time this runs.
    _sweep_codex_sessions(codex_threads)
    # Mirrors pervio-sessions.encode (every non-alphanumeric becomes '-');
    # test_fanout asserts the two stay byte-identical.
    enc = re.sub(r"[^a-zA-Z0-9]", "-", os.path.realpath(_wf_dir(wf)))
    proj_root = os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), ".claude", "projects")
    try:
        for name in os.listdir(proj_root):
            if name == enc or name.startswith(enc + "-"):
                shutil.rmtree(os.path.join(proj_root, name), ignore_errors=True)
    except OSError:
        pass


# ---------- schedules: fire a saved spec on a daily clock, no daemon ----------
#
# The OS scheduler is the dumb clock (macOS LaunchAgent StartInterval=300 / Linux
# crontab */5 line, both registered by `schedule add` and surviving reboots on
# their own — the phone plays no part); `schedule tick` is the brain. Semantics:
#   - at-most-once per due period (period key = the due occurrence's date);
#   - catch-up: after sleep/reboot/downtime, only the MOST RECENT missed period
#     fires (a digest three days late once beats three stale digests at boot);
#   - add/enable ARM to the current period (no surprise fire the moment you save);
#   - overlap guard checks the last run's pgid LIVENESS, not just its ledger text,
#     so a reboot mid-run can never wedge the schedule on a stale "running";
#   - a failing start is retried at most 3 ticks, then gives up for the period
#     with lastError kept visible;
#   - after each fire, terminal runs of this schedule beyond keepRuns are pruned
#     through the normal delete (worktrees, branches, conversations all sweep).

HOOK_LABEL = "com.pervio.schedule"
TICK_INTERVAL = 300
CRON_TAG = "# pervio-schedule"


def _sched_dir(sid):
    if not sid or not VALID_ID.match(sid):
        _die("bad schedule id")
    return os.path.join(SCHED, sid)


def _sched_now():
    """Local wall-clock 'now'; PERVIO_TICK_NOW (ISO, e.g. 2026-07-08T06:03) lets
    tests replay any moment hermetically."""
    v = os.environ.get("PERVIO_TICK_NOW")
    if v:
        return datetime.datetime.fromisoformat(v)
    return datetime.datetime.now()


def _parse_hhmm(t):
    m = re.match(r"^([01]?\d|2[0-3]):([0-5]\d)$", (t or "").strip())
    if not m:
        raise ValueError("time must be HH:MM (24h)")
    return int(m.group(1)), int(m.group(2))


def _occurrence(sch, now, direction):
    """The nearest scheduled occurrence: direction=-1 → latest one <= now (due),
    +1 → earliest one > now (next). None if `days` never matches."""
    hh, mm = _parse_hhmm(sch.get("time"))
    days = sch.get("days") or []          # [] = daily; else ISO weekdays 1-7
    for step in range(8):
        d = (now + datetime.timedelta(days=step * direction)).date()
        if days and d.isoweekday() not in days:
            continue
        cand = datetime.datetime.combine(d, datetime.time(hh, mm))
        if (direction < 0 and cand <= now) or (direction > 0 and cand > now):
            return cand
    return None


def _period_key(due):
    return due.strftime("%Y-%m-%d")


def _read_sched(sid):
    return _read_json(os.path.join(_sched_dir(sid), "schedule.json"))


def _write_sched(sid, sch):
    _write_atomic(os.path.join(_sched_dir(sid), "schedule.json"), sch)


def _merge_sched(sid, updates):
    """Field-level read-modify-write of schedule.json under a per-schedule lock:
    re-read the on-disk record, apply ONLY the given fields, write atomically. Tick
    and the phone verbs (enable/disable/fire) each touch DIFFERENT fields; without
    this a tick that read the record seconds ago would write back a stale copy and
    silently revert a concurrent disable, or restore a consumed lastFired and refire
    a period. Returns the merged record (or None if the schedule vanished)."""
    import fcntl
    sd = _sched_dir(sid)
    lockf = open(os.path.join(sd, ".sched.lock"), "w")
    try:
        fcntl.flock(lockf, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
        cur = _read_json(os.path.join(sd, "schedule.json"))
        if not cur:
            return None
        cur.update(updates)
        _write_atomic(os.path.join(sd, "schedule.json"), cur)
        return cur
    finally:
        lockf.close()


# The subset of fields tick owns — a tick write must never clobber `enabled`
# (the user's disable) or any field outside this set. _tick_claim_and_start writes
# exactly these, on a record it read under the schedule's own lock.
_TICK_FIELDS = ("lastFired", "lastFiredAt", "lastWfId", "lastError",
                "attempts", "lastAttemptPeriod")


def _all_scheds():
    out = []
    if os.path.isdir(SCHED):
        for sid in sorted(os.listdir(SCHED)):
            if not VALID_ID.match(sid):
                continue
            sch = _read_sched(sid)
            if sch:
                out.append(sch)
    return out


WF_MAX_AGE = float(os.environ.get("PERVIO_WF_MAX_AGE", str(6 * 3600)) or str(6 * 3600))


def _wf_alive(wf):
    """Is this run REALLY still running? status says running AND its process group is
    alive AND that group is provably OURS. A dead pgid with a 'running' status is a
    crash/reboot relic; and after a reboot the pgid number may be REUSED by an
    unrelated long-lived process (login/session leader) that answers signal 0. We now
    settle that with the recorded start token (_pgid_identity_ok): a match means the
    group is genuinely this run — alive no matter how long it has run, so a legit long
    workflow no longer gets declared dead and overlap-started. The 6h age cap remains
    ONLY as the fallback when identity is unprovable (an old run predating the token,
    or a platform without start times)."""
    if not wf or not VALID_ID.match(wf):
        return False
    rd = os.path.join(WF, wf)
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(rd, "status")) as f:
            if f.read().strip() != "running":
                return False
        with open(os.path.join(rd, "pgid")) as f:
            pgid = int(f.read().strip())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return False
    ident = _pgid_identity_ok(rd, pgid)
    if ident is True:
        return True                        # proven ours — alive regardless of age
    if ident is False:
        return False                       # dead, or the pid was reused
    # ident is None: can't prove identity — fall back to the age cap self-heal.
    led = _read_json(os.path.join(rd, "ledger.json"))
    started = led.get("startedAt", 0)
    if started and (time.time() - started) > WF_MAX_AGE:
        return False                       # ancient 'running' — crash relic, pgid reuse
    return True                            # alive group, recent, identity unknowable


def _last_status(sch):
    """running | done | failed | "" for the schedule's most recent run."""
    wf = sch.get("lastWfId") or ""
    if not wf or not VALID_ID.match(wf):
        return ""
    led = _read_json(os.path.join(WF, wf, "ledger.json"))
    st = led.get("status") or ""
    if st == "running" and not _wf_alive(wf):
        return "failed"                   # crashed/rebooted mid-run
    return st


# ---- the OS hook (dumb clock). Registered iff >=1 enabled schedule exists. ----

def _tick_log():
    return os.path.join(SCHED, "tick.log")


def _plist_path():
    return os.path.join(HOME, "Library", "LaunchAgents", HOOK_LABEL + ".plist")


# The name Login Items shows for the background item is ProgramArguments[0]'s
# basename. Registering the tick as a bare `python3` (sys.executable) surfaced in
# System Settings > General > Login Items as an anonymous "python3" — a background
# item the user can't attribute to anything. Register instead via a thin shell
# shim (install.sh drops it beside the launchers; it exec's python3 on this same
# script's `schedule tick`), so the login item reads `pervio-schedule-tick`.
SCHED_SHIM = "pervio-schedule-tick"


def _tick_shim():
    """The shim's absolute path (installed next to this script in ~/.pervio/bin).
    Present on any properly-installed server; may be absent when running straight
    from a checkout / the test tree — callers fall back to the interpreter then."""
    return os.path.join(SELF_DIR, SCHED_SHIM)


def _tick_argv():
    """The command that runs one tick, as an argv list. Prefer the named shim so a
    macOS Login Item is attributable (`pervio-schedule-tick`, not a bare
    `python3`); fall back to `<python3> <self> schedule tick` only when the shim
    isn't installed (a bare checkout / the tests)."""
    shim = _tick_shim()
    if os.path.isfile(shim) and os.access(shim, os.X_OK):
        return [shim]
    return [sys.executable or "/usr/bin/python3", SELF, "schedule", "tick"]


def _plist_argv0_stale():
    """True iff an installed macOS plist still names a bare `python3` as
    ProgramArguments[0] while the shim IS now available — the pre-shim registration
    that shows in Login Items as an anonymous 'python3'. A machine upgraded in place
    keeps that old plist until something rewrites it; this lets the next tick do the
    smooth swap exactly once (see _sched_tick), instead of waiting for the user to
    touch the schedule. False on non-darwin, when no plist exists, or when the
    plist already matches _tick_argv (nothing to fix)."""
    if sys.platform != "darwin":
        return False
    path = _plist_path()
    if not os.path.isfile(path):
        return False
    try:
        import plistlib
        with open(path, "rb") as f:
            argv = (plistlib.load(f) or {}).get("ProgramArguments") or []
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return False
    if not argv or argv == _tick_argv():
        return False
    return "python" in os.path.basename(str(argv[0]))


def _cron_line():
    """The crontab command line, or None if a path can't be safely encoded. cron
    treats an unescaped % in the command as end-of-command + stdin, so % must be
    backslash-escaped; a " or newline in a path can't be quoted at all → refuse."""
    exe = sys.executable or "/usr/bin/python3"
    parts = [exe, SELF, _tick_log()]
    if any('"' in p or "\n" in p for p in parts):
        return None
    exe, self_, log = (p.replace("%", r"\%") for p in parts)
    return '*/5 * * * * "%s" "%s" schedule tick >> "%s" 2>&1 %s' % (
        exe, self_, log, CRON_TAG)


def _hook_reconcile():
    """Converge the OS hook onto reality: installed exactly when an enabled
    schedule exists (no wakeups otherwise — the clock costs nothing when idle).
    PERVIO_SCHED_HOOK=skip writes/removes files but never calls launchctl/crontab
    (hermetic tests must not touch the real user's agents). Best-effort: returns
    a dict describing what happened; a hook failure never blocks the verb."""
    want = any(s.get("enabled") for s in _all_scheds())
    skip = os.environ.get("PERVIO_SCHED_HOOK", "") == "skip"
    # PERVIO_SCHED_HOOK_KIND=darwin|cron picks the hook regardless of the OS — a
    # test seam, so the launchd path (plist + launchctl shim) is exercised on a
    # Linux CI runner too. Unset = the platform's own.
    kind = os.environ.get("PERVIO_SCHED_HOOK_KIND") or ("darwin" if sys.platform == "darwin" else "cron")
    if kind == "darwin":
        return _hook_darwin(want, skip)
    return _hook_cron(want, skip)


def _hook_darwin(want, skip):
    import plistlib
    path = _plist_path()
    out = {"kind": "launchd", "installed": want, "activated": False, "note": ""}
    uid = os.getuid()

    def lctl(*args):
        try:
            return subprocess.run(["launchctl"] + list(args), stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                                  capture_output=True, text=True).returncode
        except OSError:
            return 127

    if want:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
        os.makedirs(SCHED, mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
        plist = {
            "Label": HOOK_LABEL,
            # ProgramArguments[0]'s basename is the name Login Items shows; go
            # through the named shim so it reads pervio-schedule-tick, not a bare
            # anonymous python3. (Falls back to the interpreter if the shim isn't
            # installed — see _tick_argv.)
            "ProgramArguments": _tick_argv(),
            # StartInterval proved unreliable on this launchd: the job registers
            # but the interval timer never engages (zombie — runs stayed 0 for 13h,
            # and stayed 1 for 35min even after a kickstart primed one run).
            # Wall-clock calendar entries are the battle-tested cron replacement,
            # and a window missed while asleep coalesces into ONE run on wake —
            # which is exactly the 06:30 catch-up this schedule wants.
            "StartCalendarInterval": [{"Minute": m} for m in range(0, 60, 5)],
            # RunAtLoad: one tick right at login — that's the reboot catch-up.
            "RunAtLoad": True,
            "ProcessType": "Background",
            "StandardOutPath": _tick_log(),
            "StandardErrorPath": _tick_log(),
        }
        tmp = path + ".tmp"
        with open(tmp, "wb") as f:
            plistlib.dump(plist, f)
        os.replace(tmp, path)
        if not skip:
            lctl("bootout", "gui/%d/%s" % (uid, HOOK_LABEL))       # re-load fresh
            time.sleep(1)      # bootout is async; racing the re-bootstrap of the
                               # same label is one way to mint a zombie registration
            if lctl("bootstrap", "gui/%d" % uid, path) == 0:
                out["activated"] = True
            elif lctl("load", "-w", path) == 0:                    # older launchctl
                out["activated"] = True
            else:
                out["note"] = ("registered for next login (launchctl couldn't "
                               "activate it from this session)")
            if out["activated"]:
                # Prime the job. A bootout immediately followed by a bootstrap of
                # the same label can leave a ZOMBIE registration: launchctl print
                # shows the job, yet runs stays 0 forever — neither RunAtLoad nor
                # StartInterval ever fires (observed 2026-07-21: 13h loaded, zero
                # executions, first 06:30 digest silently missed). One kickstart
                # un-wedges it, and tick is at-most-once per period, so an extra
                # run is always harmless. `start` is the pre-10.11 spelling.
                if lctl("kickstart", "gui/%d/%s" % (uid, HOOK_LABEL)) != 0:
                    lctl("start", HOOK_LABEL)
    else:
        if not skip:
            lctl("bootout", "gui/%d/%s" % (uid, HOOK_LABEL))
        try:
            os.remove(path)
        except OSError:
            pass
    return out


def _hook_cron(want, skip):
    out = {"kind": "cron", "installed": want, "activated": False, "note": ""}
    if skip:
        return out
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(["crontab", "-l"], stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                           capture_output=True, text=True)
        # `crontab -l` exits non-zero BOTH for the benign "no crontab yet" case and
        # for real failures (permission/spool errors). Treating the latter as "empty"
        # and then writing our line would DESTROY the user's existing cron jobs. Only
        # proceed on rc==0, or the well-known empty-crontab message; on anything else
        # bail without touching the table.
        if p.returncode == 0:
            cur = p.stdout
        elif re.search(r"no crontab", (p.stderr or ""), re.I):
            cur = ""
        else:
            out["note"] = "left crontab untouched: 'crontab -l' failed (%s)" % (
                (p.stderr or "").strip()[:80] or "rc %d" % p.returncode)
            return out
        kept = [l for l in cur.splitlines() if CRON_TAG not in l]
        had_ours = any(CRON_TAG in l for l in cur.splitlines())
        lines = list(kept)
        if want:
            os.makedirs(SCHED, mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
            line = _cron_line()
            if line is None:
                out["note"] = ("launcher path has characters cron can't quote "
                               "(\" or newline) — not installed")
                return out
            lines.append(line)
        elif not had_ours:
            out["activated"] = False       # nothing of ours to remove; don't rewrite
            return out
        content = ("\n".join(lines) + "\n") if lines else ""
        # Empty content would `crontab -` an empty table; only do that if the user
        # actually had a crontab we're now emptying of our own line (kept==[]).
        q = subprocess.run(["crontab", "-"], input=content, text=True,
                           capture_output=True)
        if q.returncode == 0:
            out["activated"] = want
        else:
            out["note"] = "crontab failed: %s" % (q.stderr or "").strip()[:120]
    except OSError as e:
        out["note"] = "crontab unavailable: %s" % e
    return out


# ---- verbs ----

def _sched_add(argv):
    o = _parse(argv, {"b64"})
    if "b64" not in o:
        _die("schedule add needs --b64")
    try:
        payload = json.loads(base64.b64decode(o["b64"]).decode("utf-8"))
        hh_mm = payload.get("time") or ""
        _parse_hhmm(hh_mm)
        days = payload.get("days") or []
        if not isinstance(days, list) or any(not isinstance(d, int) or not 1 <= d <= 7
                                             for d in days):
            raise ValueError("days must be ISO weekday ints 1-7")
        keep = max(1, int(payload.get("keepRuns", 7) or 7))   # validate BEFORE mkdir
        if "enabled" in payload and not isinstance(payload["enabled"], bool):
            raise ValueError("enabled must be a boolean")       # 0 is not False: it used to save switched ON
        spec = payload.get("spec") or {}
        _validate_spec(spec)
    except Exception as e:
        _die("bad schedule: %s" % e)
    os.makedirs(SCHED, mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
    sid = uuid.uuid4().hex[:12]
    sd = _sched_dir(sid)
    os.makedirs(sd, mode=0o700)
    _write_atomic(os.path.join(sd, "spec.json"), spec)
    now = _sched_now()
    sch = {
        "id": sid,
        "name": payload.get("name") or spec.get("name") or "",
        # The phone that added it (its push device id + server entry): every run
        # this schedule fires tells that phone only (2026-10-18).
        "device": str(payload.get("device") or ""),
        "origin": str(payload.get("origin") or ""),
        "time": hh_mm,
        "days": days,
        "keepRuns": keep,
        # Switched on unless the caller says otherwise: pervio-mcp saves one
        # PAUSED when the conversation that asked for it shows nobody the
        # request first (2026-11-21).
        "enabled": payload.get("enabled") is not False,
        "createdAt": int(time.time()),
        # Armed: the current period counts as already fired, so the first real
        # fire is the NEXT occurrence — never the moment you tap Save.
        "lastFired": _armed_period(sch_time=hh_mm, days=days, now=now),
        "lastFiredAt": 0,
        "lastWfId": "",
        "lastError": "",
        "attempts": 0,
        "lastAttemptPeriod": "",
    }
    _write_sched(sid, sch)
    hook = _hook_reconcile()
    sys.stdout.write(json.dumps({"id": sid, "hook": hook}) + "\n")


def _armed_period(sch_time, days, now):
    due = _occurrence({"time": sch_time, "days": days}, now, -1)
    return _period_key(due) if due else ""


def _sched_update(argv):
    """In-place edit of a saved schedule (the id never changes):
    {"id": required, "time"?: "HH:MM", "days"?: [1-7], "keepRuns"?: int,
     "enabled"?: bool, "spec"?: {}} — only the given fields move. A time/days
    change RE-ARMS the clock (same no-surprise-fire rule as add/enable: the new
    time's current period counts as consumed, the first fire is its next real
    occurrence) and reconciles the OS hook, as does an enabled flip; a
    keepRuns/spec-only edit touches neither. Prints the updated schedule record
    with nextDue recomputed under the new clock."""
    o = _parse(argv, {"b64"})
    if "b64" not in o:
        _die("schedule update needs --b64")
    try:
        payload = json.loads(base64.b64decode(o["b64"]).decode("utf-8"))
        if not isinstance(payload, dict):
            raise ValueError("payload must be a JSON object")
    except Exception as e:
        _die("bad update: %s" % e)
    sid = str(payload.get("id") or "")
    cur = _read_sched(sid)                 # _sched_dir validates the id shape
    if not cur:
        _die("no such schedule", 3)
    upd = {}
    spec = None
    try:                                   # validate EVERYTHING before writing anything
        if "time" in payload:
            _parse_hhmm(payload.get("time"))
            upd["time"] = payload["time"]
        if "days" in payload:
            days = payload.get("days") or []
            if not isinstance(days, list) or any(not isinstance(d, int) or not 1 <= d <= 7
                                                 for d in days):
                raise ValueError("days must be ISO weekday ints 1-7")
            upd["days"] = days
        if "keepRuns" in payload:
            upd["keepRuns"] = max(1, int(payload.get("keepRuns") or 7))
        if "enabled" in payload:
            # `x in (True, False)` lets 1 and 0 through (1 == True), and the
            # caller above this one had read 1 as "not switching it on".
            if not isinstance(payload.get("enabled"), bool):
                raise ValueError("enabled must be a boolean")
            upd["enabled"] = payload["enabled"]
        spec = payload.get("spec")
        if spec is not None:
            _validate_spec(spec)
    except Exception as e:
        _die("bad update: %s" % e)
    clock_changed = (("time" in upd and upd["time"] != cur.get("time"))
                     or ("days" in upd and upd["days"] != (cur.get("days") or [])))
    re_enabled = upd.get("enabled") is True and not cur.get("enabled")
    if clock_changed or re_enabled:
        # Re-arm under the NEW clock: periods the new time/days would place in
        # the past (or periods missed while paused) never fire on save.
        upd["lastFired"] = _armed_period(upd.get("time", cur.get("time")),
                                         upd.get("days", cur.get("days") or []),
                                         _sched_now())
        upd["attempts"] = 0
    # An update that both replaces the spec and switches the schedule off must
    # never leave new content under an armed clock, not even for the moment
    # between two writes (the scheduler reads `enabled`, then spec.json): off
    # first, then the new spec. Any other update keeps spec-then-record, so a
    # schedule being switched ON is switched on over its final spec.
    merged = None
    if upd.get("enabled") is False:
        merged = _merge_sched(sid, upd)
        if merged is None:
            _die("no such schedule", 3)
    if spec is not None:
        _write_atomic(os.path.join(_sched_dir(sid), "spec.json"), spec)
    if merged is None:
        merged = _merge_sched(sid, upd) if upd else cur
    if merged is None:
        _die("no such schedule", 3)
    if clock_changed or "enabled" in upd:
        _hook_reconcile()                  # best-effort, like every other verb
    row = dict(merged)
    try:
        nxt = _occurrence(merged, _sched_now(), +1)
    except ValueError:
        nxt = None
    row["nextDue"] = int(nxt.timestamp()) if nxt else 0
    sys.stdout.write(json.dumps(row) + "\n")


def _sched_list(argv):
    o = _parse(argv, {"device"})
    now = _sched_now()
    out = []
    for sch in _all_scheds():
        # --device: this phone's own schedules (2026-10-19); one that recorded
        # no device (older app, the shell) stays visible to everyone.
        if o.get("device") and sch.get("device") and sch.get("device") != o["device"]:
            continue
        row = dict(sch)
        try:
            nxt = _occurrence(sch, now, +1)
        except ValueError:
            nxt = None
        row["nextDue"] = int(nxt.timestamp()) if nxt else 0
        row["lastStatus"] = _last_status(sch)
        row["tz"] = datetime.datetime.now().astimezone().strftime("%z")
        out.append(row)
    sys.stdout.write(json.dumps(out) + "\n")


def _sched_remove(argv):
    o = _parse(argv, {"id"})
    sd = _sched_dir(o.get("id", ""))
    if not os.path.isdir(sd):
        _die("no such schedule", 3)
    shutil.rmtree(sd, ignore_errors=True)
    residue = _residue(sd)                 # ignore_errors hides a denied unlink
    hook = _hook_reconcile()               # last one out turns the clock off
    if residue:
        sys.stderr.write("pervio-orchestrate: schedule remove of %s did NOT finish — "
                         "still on disk:\n%s\n"
                         % (o["id"], "\n".join("  " + r for r in residue)))
        sys.stdout.write(json.dumps({"removed": False, "id": o["id"], "reason": "residue",
                                     "remaining": residue, "hook": hook}) + "\n")
        sys.exit(1)
    sys.stdout.write(json.dumps({"removed": o["id"], "hook": hook}) + "\n")


def _sched_set_enabled(argv, enabled):
    o = _parse(argv, {"id"})
    sid = o.get("id", "")
    if not _read_sched(sid):
        _die("no such schedule", 3)
    upd = {"enabled": enabled}
    if enabled:
        # Re-arm: periods missed while disabled don't fire on re-enable.
        upd["lastFired"] = _armed_period(_read_sched(sid).get("time"),
                                         _read_sched(sid).get("days"), _sched_now())
        upd["attempts"] = 0
    _merge_sched(sid, upd)                  # only touch these fields (never tick's)
    hook = _hook_reconcile()
    sys.stdout.write(json.dumps({"id": sid, "enabled": enabled, "hook": hook}) + "\n")


def _sched_owner(sid, extra):
    """`extra` plus the phone that owns schedule `sid` (device + origin recorded
    at add time), so a fired run's pushes go to that phone only."""
    try:
        sch = _read_sched(sid)
    except Exception:
        sch = {}
    for k in ("device", "origin"):
        if sch.get(k):
            extra[k] = sch[k]
    return extra


def _sched_fire(argv):
    """Fire NOW, regardless of the clock — the 'test it once' button. Does not
    consume the period (the scheduled occurrence still runs), but does set
    lastWfId so the overlap guard sees it."""
    o = _parse(argv, {"id"})
    sid = o.get("id", "")
    sch = _read_sched(sid)
    if not sch:
        _die("no such schedule", 3)
    spec = _read_json(os.path.join(_sched_dir(sid), "spec.json"))
    try:
        _validate_spec(spec)
        wid = _start_wf(spec, extra=_sched_owner(sid, {"scheduleId": sid}))
    except Exception as e:
        _die("fire failed: %s" % e)
    # Merge ONLY fire's fields — never lastFired (fire doesn't consume the period),
    # so a tick that just wrote lastFired isn't clobbered back to a stale value.
    _merge_sched(sid, {"lastWfId": wid, "lastFiredAt": int(time.time()), "lastError": ""})
    _prune_sched_runs(sid, int(sch.get("keepRuns", 7) or 7))
    sys.stdout.write(json.dumps({"id": sid, "wfId": wid}) + "\n")


def _prune_sched_runs(sid, keep):
    """Keep this schedule's newest `keep` runs, delete the older TERMINAL ones —
    through the normal delete, so conversations sweep too. A still-running run is
    never deleted; since a fire prunes while its OWN just-started run is running,
    the count can transiently be keep+1 (that run is over budget but live) — the
    next day's prune, with it now terminal, brings it back to keep. Bounded, never
    a leak. (Real schedules fire ~24h apart; same-second fires would only collide
    the startedAt sort, not the bound.)"""
    runs = []
    if os.path.isdir(WF):
        for wid in os.listdir(WF):
            led = _read_json(os.path.join(WF, wid, "ledger.json"))
            if led.get("scheduleId") == sid and led.get("status"):
                runs.append((led.get("startedAt", 0), wid, led.get("status")))
    runs.sort(reverse=True)
    # A 'running' corpse (runner killed hard) used to be immune to pruning
    # forever. Settle its status first; a truly live run stays protected.
    runs = [(ts, wid,
             st if st != "running" or _wf_alive(wid)
             else _reconcile_dead(_wf_dir(wid),
                                  _read_json(os.path.join(WF, wid, "ledger.json"))
                                  ).get("status", "failed"))
            for ts, wid, st in runs]
    kept = 0
    for _, wid, st in runs:
        if st == "running" or kept < max(1, keep):
            kept += 1
            continue
        try:
            _delete_run(wid, _wf_dir(wid))
        except Exception:
            pass                           # pruning must never break the tick


def _tick_claim_and_start(sid, period):
    """ORDERING IS DELIBERATE — start the run FIRST, claim the period AFTER.
    The 2026-08-22 audit flagged the crash window between the two (kernel kill
    mid-lock → run started, period unclaimed → next tick fires the period
    again) and suggested claiming first. Rejected: claim-then-crash means the
    period is consumed and NOTHING ran — the digest silently never happens and
    nothing retries it. A microsecond window that can duplicate one issue
    (same-day publish overwrites anyway) beats one that can silently skip a
    day. The PAID part of a tick, done entirely inside the schedule's own lock — the
    same lock every phone verb (enable / disable / update / fire) takes through
    _merge_sched. Re-reads the record, re-checks that it is still enabled and that
    this period is still unclaimed, starts the run, and claims the period, all in ONE
    critical section. Before this the decision and the claim straddled an unlocked
    window: a pause (or a remove) that landed inside it was invisible, the run started
    anyway, and the user paid for a schedule they had just turned off.

    Never blocks: a contended lock means a phone verb is mid-update, so the schedule is
    left for the next tick — nothing is written, the period stays unconsumed, and five
    minutes later it fires normally. Returns
    ("fired", wfId) | ("busy", "") | ("skip", "") | ("error", message)."""
    import fcntl
    sd = _sched_dir(sid)
    try:
        lockf = open(os.path.join(sd, ".sched.lock"), "w")
    except OSError as e:
        return "error", str(e)[:300]
    try:
        try:
            fcntl.flock(lockf, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
        except OSError:
            return "busy", ""
        # Authoritative re-reads, INSIDE the lock: the loop's snapshot was taken
        # before _wf_alive, which can block on ps/killpg for seconds.
        cur = _read_json(os.path.join(sd, "schedule.json"))
        if not cur or not cur.get("enabled"):
            return "skip", ""                  # disabled or removed since the snapshot
        last = cur.get("lastFired") or ""
        if last and last >= period:
            return "skip", ""                  # already paid for this period
        if cur.get("lastAttemptPeriod") == period and cur.get("attempts", 0) >= 3:
            return "skip", ""
        spec = _read_json(os.path.join(sd, "spec.json"))
        try:
            _validate_spec(spec)
            wid = _start_wf(spec, extra=_sched_owner(sid, {"scheduleId": sid}))
        except Exception as e:
            cur.update({"attempts": (cur.get("attempts", 0) + 1
                                     if cur.get("lastAttemptPeriod") == period else 1),
                        "lastAttemptPeriod": period, "lastError": str(e)[:300]})
            _write_atomic(os.path.join(sd, "schedule.json"), cur)
            return "error", str(e)[:300]
        # Claim in the SAME critical section that started it (the record was read
        # under this lock, so writing it whole cannot clobber a concurrent verb).
        cur.update({"lastFired": period, "lastFiredAt": int(time.time()),
                    "lastWfId": wid, "lastError": "", "attempts": 0,
                    "lastAttemptPeriod": period})
        _write_atomic(os.path.join(sd, "schedule.json"), cur)
        return "fired", wid
    finally:
        lockf.close()


def _sched_tick(argv):
    """The brain. Silent no-op when nothing is due (the log stays tiny); prints
    one JSON line naming what fired. Serialized by a non-blocking flock so a
    slow tick can't stack up behind the 5-minute clock."""
    import fcntl
    os.makedirs(SCHED, mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
    lockf = open(os.path.join(SCHED, ".tick.lock"), "w")
    try:
        fcntl.flock(lockf, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
    except OSError:
        return                             # a previous tick is still at it
    # In-place upgrade self-heal: a machine that registered before the shim existed
    # still runs an old plist whose ProgramArguments[0] is a bare `python3` (an
    # anonymous Login Item). Rewrite + re-bootstrap it ONCE, the first tick that
    # sees the shim now available — not every tick (that would bootout/bootstrap the
    # job on the 5-minute clock forever). After the swap _plist_argv0_stale is False.
    if _plist_argv0_stale():
        try:
            _hook_reconcile()
        except Exception:
            pass                           # never let a hook re-register stall a tick
    now = _sched_now()
    fired = []
    for sch in _all_scheds():
        sid = sch.get("id", "")
        try:
            if not sch.get("enabled"):
                continue
            due = _occurrence(sch, now, -1)
            if not due:
                continue
            period = _period_key(due)
            # Consumed-period guard, order-aware: skip if this exact period ran, OR
            # if a LATER period already ran (the wall clock moved backward — westward
            # travel / NTP / manual correction — and `due` is now an old occurrence
            # we already covered). Equality alone would refire it.
            last = sch.get("lastFired") or ""
            if last and last >= period:
                continue
            if sch.get("lastAttemptPeriod") == period and sch.get("attempts", 0) >= 3:
                continue                   # gave up on this period (lastError says why)
            if _wf_alive(sch.get("lastWfId")):
                continue                   # previous run still going — retry next tick
            # Everything above is a cheap FILTER on a snapshot; the authoritative
            # re-read, the launch and the period claim happen together under the
            # schedule's lock, so a DISABLE can never land between them (remove does not take this lock — a delete racing a tick can still start one final run).
            outcome, detail = _tick_claim_and_start(sid, period)
            if outcome != "fired":
                continue                   # busy / disabled / already claimed / error
            fired.append({"id": sid, "wfId": detail, "period": period})
            _prune_sched_runs(sid, int(sch.get("keepRuns", 7) or 7))
        except Exception as e:             # one broken schedule must not stall the rest
            try:
                _merge_sched(sid, {"lastError": str(e)[:300]})
            except Exception:
                pass
    if fired:
        sys.stdout.write(json.dumps({"tick": now.isoformat(timespec="minutes"),
                                     "fired": fired}) + "\n")


def cmd_schedule(argv):
    subs = {"add": _sched_add, "update": _sched_update, "list": _sched_list,
            "remove": _sched_remove,
            "enable": lambda a: _sched_set_enabled(a, True),
            "disable": lambda a: _sched_set_enabled(a, False),
            "fire": _sched_fire, "tick": _sched_tick,
            "install": lambda a: sys.stdout.write(json.dumps(_hook_reconcile()) + "\n"),
            "uninstall": lambda a: sys.stdout.write(json.dumps(_hook_reconcile()) + "\n")}
    if not argv or argv[0] not in subs:
        _die("usage: pervio-orchestrate schedule "
             "{add|update|list|remove|enable|disable|fire|tick|install|uninstall} …")
    subs[argv[0]](argv[1:])


def cmd_teststep(argv):
    """Iterate ONE step against a PRIOR run's recorded inputs (WF-3): the step
    definition comes from --spec-b64 (your edited spec) or the old run's own,
    the {{ref}} inputs come from the old ledger/json artifacts, and the turn
    runs through run_child's ephemeral worktree — writes never land. Fan-out
    steps test a single child (--item N, default 0). Prints the result and the
    outputSchema verdict; exit 0 only when both are good. Prompt iteration on
    step 19 of a 40-minute pipeline becomes a 2-minute loop.

    Command steps are refused: they are deterministic — run the line yourself
    with PERVIO_WF_DIR pointed at the old run."""
    o = _parse(argv, {"wf", "step", "item", "spec-b64"})
    rd = _wf_dir(o.get("wf", ""))
    if not os.path.isdir(rd):
        _die("no workflow %s" % o.get("wf", ""))
    old_led = _read_json(os.path.join(rd, "ledger.json"))
    spec = _read_json(os.path.join(rd, "spec.json"))
    if "spec-b64" in o:
        try:
            spec = json.loads(base64.b64decode(o["spec-b64"]).decode("utf-8"))
        except Exception as e:
            _die("bad spec: %s" % e)
        try:
            _validate_spec(spec)
        except ValueError as e:
            _die(str(e))
    sid = o.get("step", "")
    s = next((x for x in spec.get("steps", []) if x.get("id") == sid), None)
    if not s:
        _die("no step %r in the spec" % sid)
    if s.get("command"):
        _die("step %s is a command step — deterministic, so run it yourself:\n"
             "  PERVIO_WF_DIR=%s bash -lc <the command>" % (sid, rd))

    # The old run's outputs are this test's inputs — done steps only.
    snap, snap_json = {}, {}
    for st in old_led.get("steps", []):
        if st.get("status") == "done":
            snap[st.get("id", "")] = st.get("output", "")
    jd = os.path.join(rd, "json")
    if os.path.isdir(jd):
        for name in os.listdir(jd):
            if name.endswith(".json"):
                try:
                    with open(os.path.join(jd, name)) as f:
                        snap_json[name[:-5]] = json.load(f)
                except (OSError, ValueError):
                    pass

    fo = s.get("fanout")
    keep = set(RESERVED_IDS)
    if fo:
        keep.add(fo.get("as", "item"))
    prompt = _resolve_refs(s.get("prompt", ""), snap, snap_json, keep_ids=keep)
    item_label = ""
    if fo:
        over = fo.get("over", "") or ""
        mj = JSON_REF_RE.search(over)
        if mj:
            src_v = snap_json.get(mj.group(1))
            if not isinstance(src_v, list):
                _die("fanout source %s has no structured array in run %s"
                     % (over, o.get("wf")))
            items = src_v
        else:
            m = REF_RE.search(over)
            txt = snap.get(m.group(1), "") if m else ""
            if not txt.strip():
                _die("fanout source %s is empty in run %s" % (over, o.get("wf")))
            try:
                items = _fanout_split(fo.get("split", "numbered"), txt)
            except (ValueError, TypeError) as e:
                _die("fanout split failed: %s" % e)
        try:
            idx = int(o.get("item", "0") or "0")
        except ValueError:
            _die("--item must be an integer")
        if idx < 0 or idx >= len(items):
            _die("item %d out of range (this run split %d items)" % (idx, len(items)))
        x = items[idx]
        as_var = fo.get("as", "item")

        def key_sub(mm):
            if isinstance(x, dict):
                vv = x.get(mm.group(1))
                if vv is None:
                    return ""
                return vv if isinstance(vv, str) else _json_text(vv)
            return ""
        prompt = re.compile(r"\{\{\s*" + re.escape(as_var)
                            + r"\.([A-Za-z0-9_-]+)\s*\}\}").sub(key_sub, prompt)
        prompt = re.compile(r"\{\{\s*" + re.escape(as_var) + r"\s*\}\}").sub(
            lambda _m: x if isinstance(x, str) else _json_text(x), prompt)
        item_label = " [item %d]" % idx

    base = os.path.join(rd, "teststep")
    os.makedirs(base, mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
    n = 1 + max([int(d) for d in os.listdir(base) if d.isdigit()] or [0])
    tdir = os.path.join(base, str(n))
    backend = s.get("backend", "claude")
    step_dir = os.path.realpath(_expand_dir(s.get("dir", spec.get("dir", "~"))))
    rc_, top, _ = _git(step_dir, "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel")
    top = os.path.realpath(top) if rc_ == 0 and top else None
    requested = s.get("sandbox", "read-only") if backend == "codex" else s.get("perm", "plan")
    try:
        eff = clamp_tier(backend, requested, backend, requested)
    except DispatchError as de:
        _die("tier: %s" % de.detail)
    if fo:
        try:
            child_timeout = float(fo.get("timeoutSec") or 0)
        except (TypeError, ValueError):
            child_timeout = 0.0
        timeout = (min(max(child_timeout, 60.0), 900.0) if child_timeout > 0
                   else CHILD_TIMEOUT)
    else:
        timeout = AGENT_TIMEOUT
    b64 = base64.b64encode(prompt.encode("utf-8")).decode("ascii")
    branch = None if top is None else "pervio/wf-%s/teststep/%d" % (o.get("wf"), n)
    t0 = time.time()
    try:
        ok, text, _cost, err = run_child(b64, backend, eff, top, tdir, branch, 0,
                                         timeout, CHAT, resolve_model(s.get("model")),
                                         s.get("effort"), None,
                                         bool(s.get("allowNetwork")))
    except DispatchError as de:
        _die("child failed to start: %s" % de.detail)
    verdict = ""
    if ok and s.get("outputSchema") is not None:
        _val, verr = _parse_and_validate(s["outputSchema"], text)
        verdict = "schema: ok" if not verr else "schema: FAIL — %s" % verr
        if verr:
            ok = False
    lines = ["teststep %s/%s%s  (%.0fs, 一次性 worktree,写入不落地)"
             % (o.get("wf"), sid, item_label, time.time() - t0),
             "result: %s%s" % ("OK" if ok else "FAIL", (" — " + err) if err else "")]
    if verdict:
        lines.append(verdict)
    sys.stdout.write("\n".join(lines) + "\n---\n" + (text or "") + "\n")
    _write_atomic(os.path.join(tdir, "result.json"),
                  {"ok": ok, "step": sid, "item": o.get("item"),
                   "error": err, "schema": verdict, "text": text})
    sys.exit(0 if ok else 1)


CMDS = {"start": cmd_start, "resolve": cmd_resolve, "run": cmd_run, "status": cmd_status,
        "spec": cmd_spec, "tail": cmd_tail, "list": cmd_list, "delete": cmd_delete,
        "teststep": cmd_teststep,
        "schedule": cmd_schedule, "templates": cmd_templates, "archives": cmd_archives,
        "models": cmd_models}


def main():
    if len(sys.argv) < 2 or sys.argv[1] not in CMDS:
        _die("usage: pervio-orchestrate {start|status|tail|list|delete|schedule} …")
    CMDS[sys.argv[1]](sys.argv[2:])


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
